window.SD_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"id": "JXO-TPZ-EKP-ARK", "page": {"id": "JXO-TPZ-EKP-ARK", "name": "Introduction", "path": "/en/getting-started/introduction", "slug": "introduction", "tags": ["prestashop"], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "JXO-TPZ-EKP-ARK", "slug": "introduction", "title": "Introduction", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 23:16:30", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/getting-started", "content": " Klick-Zen - Actionable Buttons for Zendesk \n \n Klick-Zen adds functional, one-click action buttons to your Zendesk ticket emails, agent responses, and Help Center. When a customer clicks a button, Klick-Zen performs the actions you've assigned - updating the ticket status, adding tags, inserting a comment, changing priority, and more - without requiring the customer to log in, reply, or navigate your support portal. \n The result: faster resolutions, cleaner ticket data, and a customer experience that feels effortless. Klick-Zen hosts everything for you (button images and a customizable post-click landing page) so there's no additional hosting to set up or maintain. \n \n This documentation walks you through installing the app, creating your first button, embedding buttons throughout your Zendesk workflows, and customizing the experience to match your brand. \n \n New to Klick-Zen? Start with the How Klick-Zen Works section. \n Ready to install and configure Klick-Zen? Go directly to the Installation section. \n Installed already and want to get right to the good stuff? Check out the quick start . \n \n Need help? \n If you need additional help, please contact us at support@klick-zen.com . \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "E1C-DLY-GS1-WUV", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/getting-started", "name": "Getting Started"}, {"url": "/en/getting-started/introduction", "name": "Introduction"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Klick-Zen - Actionable Buttons for Zendesk", "Need help?", "prestashop"], "pageName": "Introduction", "pathName": "Getting-started > Introduction", "parentName": "Getting Started", "relativePath": "/en/getting-started/introduction"}, {"id": "US8-A60X-CNV-UIP", "page": {"id": "US8-A60X-CNV-UIP", "name": "How Klick-Zen Works", "path": "/en/getting-started/how-klick-zen-works", "slug": "how-klick-zen-works", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "US8-A60X-CNV-UIP", "slug": "how-klick-zen-works", "title": "How Klick-Zen Works", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-05 14:43:56", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/getting-started", "content": " How Klick-Zen Works \n Klick-Zen buttons look simple to your customers: a styled button in an email that they click once. Behind that click is a coordinated system of three components; an HTML snippet embedded in your Zendesk emails, a set of Klick-Zen hosted assets (button images and the landing page), and the Klick-Zen action service , which securely applies your configured actions to the ticket via the Zendesk API. \n This below explains how those pieces fit together, what actually gets inserted into an email, and exactly what happens the moment a customer clicks. \n \n The HTML Snippet \n When you create a button, Klick-Zen generates a small block of HTML. At its core, the snippet is simply a hyperlink wrapped around a hosted button image : \n \n An anchor ( &lt;a&gt; ) element whose href points to a unique Klick-Zen action URL for that button \n An image ( &lt;img&gt; ) element whose src points to the button graphic, rendered and hosted by Klick-Zen \n \n Because the button is delivered as a real image rather than styled HTML/CSS, it displays consistently across email clients . Gmail, Outlook, Apple Mail, and mobile apps all show the same button, with no broken styling or fallback rendering. \n You paste this snippet into any Zendesk email surface: a trigger or automation email template, a macro, or an individual ticket reply (via the Klick-Zen toolbar button in the ticket editor). \n \n Zendesk Placeholders: Connecting the Button to the Ticket \n A button is only useful if Klick-Zen knows which ticket to act on. This is where Zendesk placeholders come in. \n The action URL inside the snippet includes Zendesk placeholder syntax, the same double-curly-brace variables used throughout Zendesk email templates, such as {{ticket.id}} . In the template, the snippet's link looks similar to this: \n &lt;a href=\"https://buttons.klick-zen.com/ext/href/f2a782f8/{{ticket.id}}/{{ticket.encoded.id}}\"&gt;\n &lt;img src=\"https://buttons.klick-zen.com/ext/src/f2a782f8/{{ticket.id}}\" /&gt;\n&lt;/a&gt; \n The bottom line: the snippet you paste into a template is generic, but every email sent from it is ticket-specific. At send time, Zendesk's notification engine substitutes each placeholder with the real value for that ticket. The email that lands in the customer's inbox therefore contains a fully resolved link, one that references a specific ticket (for example, ticket 45821 ) and uniquely ties that button, in that email, to that customer's ticket. \n This is why the same snippet can live in a trigger that fires thousands of times; Zendesk personalizes the link on every send, with no per-ticket work on your part. \n \n The Klick-Zen Action Service \n The action URL doesn't point at Zendesk - it points at Klick-Zen. When your customer clicks, their browser makes a request to Klick-Zen's servers, which hold everything needed to complete the update: \n \n The button definition , which is the list of actions you configured (update status, add tags, insert a comment, assign, change priority or type) \n The authorized connection to your Zendesk instance, established when you installed and authorized the app \n \n Klick-Zen uses that authorized connection to apply your actions through the Zendesk API on the ticket identified in the link. The customer never has to log in to your help center, and no credentials are ever exposed in the email. \n \n What Gets Inserted Into an Automated Email \n Putting it together, here's the lifecycle of a button inside a Zendesk trigger email: \n \n You paste the snippet once. The Klick-Zen HTML (the anchor link with its placeholder-bearing action URL, plus the hosted image) goes into the email body of a trigger, automation, or macro. \n A ticket event fires the trigger. For example, an agent solves a ticket and the \"Notify requester\" trigger runs. \n Zendesk renders the template. All placeholders in the email, including the ones inside the Klick-Zen URL, are replaced with that ticket's live values. The generic snippet becomes a ticket-specific link. \n The email is delivered. The customer's email client fetches the button image from Klick-Zen's servers and displays it inline. To the customer, it's just a clean, branded button embedded within the message. \n \n Nothing about the ticket is exposed in the button itself beyond what's encoded in the link, and the image is identical for every recipient. Only the link is personalized. \n \n What Happens When the Button Is Clicked \n The click sets off a fast, fully automated sequence: \n \n The browser opens the action URL. Clicking the button issues a standard HTTPS request to Klick-Zen, carrying the button identifier and the ticket reference that Zendesk substituted at send time. \n Klick-Zen validates the request. The service confirms the link corresponds to a real button in your account and a valid ticket reference before doing anything else. \n The configured actions execute. Klick-Zen calls the Zendesk API over your authorized connection and applies every action assigned to the button in a single pass. One click can, for example, set the ticket to Solved , apply a customer_confirmed tag, and add a private comment noting that the customer closed the ticket via email. \n Zendesk business rules react. Because the update arrives through the Zendesk API, it is indistinguishable from any other ticket update. That means your existing triggers and automations evaluate it normally . A tag added by a button click can route the ticket, notify a supervisor, or send a follow-up email, exactly as if an agent had made the change. This trigger cascade is what makes Klick-Zen composable with the rest of your Zendesk workflow. \n \n The customer is redirected. With the actions applied, Klick-Zen immediately redirects the browser to one of two destinations: \n \n Your Klick-Zen hosted landing page : a confirmation page customized with your colors, logo, and message, served by Klick-Zen with no hosting on your side; or \n A URL you designate : your own support site, a knowledge base article, a survey, or any other page appropriate to the workflow. \n \n \n \n From the customer's perspective, the whole exchange is click, then confirmation page . From your system's perspective, the ticket has been updated, tagged, routed, and any downstream automations have already fired, all in the time it takes the landing page to load. \n \n Hosting: What Klick-Zen Serves So You Don't Have To \n Two pieces of infrastructure that would normally require your own web hosting are provided by Klick-Zen out of the box: \n \n Button images. Every button you design is rendered to an image and served from Klick-Zen's servers. Email clients load it like any other remote image. \n The landing page. The post-click confirmation page is hosted and served by Klick-Zen, with your branding applied. \n \n For a fully white-labeled experience, both can be served from a custom subdomain on your own domain (e.g., go.yourcompany.com ), so customers never see a third-party address in the email link, the image source, or the landing page URL. \n \n Customizing or Replacing the Snippet \n The generated HTML is a starting point, not a requirement. Because the mechanism is ultimately just a link, you can adapt it: \n \n Use your own image. If you host your own graphics, swap the &lt;img&gt; source for your image while keeping the Klick-Zen action URL in the anchor. The actions fire exactly the same. \n Use a plain text link. Prefer a hyperlinked sentence over a button? Remove the image entirely and wrap your text in the anchor instead. \n Place multiple buttons. Paste two or more snippets side by side to present a choice. Each button carries its own action URL and its own set of actions. \n Use your own landing page. After the button is clicked, you can redirect the user to your own web site, or to your Zendesk Help Center instead of the Klick-Zen hosted landing page. \n \n The only essential element is the action link with its placeholder intact; everything around it is presentation. \n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "E1C-DLY-GS1-WUV", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/getting-started", "name": "Getting Started"}, {"url": "/en/getting-started/how-klick-zen-works", "name": "How Klick-Zen Works"}], "last_updated": "Oct 5, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How Klick-Zen Works", "The HTML Snippet", "Zendesk Placeholders: Connecting the Button to the Ticket", "The Klick-Zen Action Service", "What Gets Inserted Into an Automated Email", "What Happens When the Button Is Clicked", "Hosting: What Klick-Zen Serves So You Don't Have To", "Customizing or Replacing the Snippet"], "pageName": "How Klick-Zen Works", "pathName": "Getting-started > How-klick-zen-works", "parentName": "Getting Started", "relativePath": "/en/getting-started/how-klick-zen-works"}, {"id": "ZW9-GR0J-61S-YL6", "page": {"id": "ZW9-GR0J-61S-YL6", "name": "Installation", "path": "/en/getting-started/installation", "slug": "installation", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "ZW9-GR0J-61S-YL6", "slug": "installation", "title": "Installation", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-31 18:14:20", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/getting-started", "content": " Installation \n Installation of Klick-Zen is fast and easy! Follow these steps and you'll be up and running in no time. \n \n \n Log into Zendesk with an administrator-level account \n \n \n Navigate to Klick-Zen in the Zendesk Marketplace and follow the installation prompts to initiate the installation: https://www.zendesk.com/marketplace/apps/support/1097296/klick-zen-action-buttons/ \n \n For more details on working with the Zendesk Marketplace, please see the Zendesk documentation located at https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408824421146-Using-the-Zendesk-Marketplace#topic_n2x_hzw_15 \n While the Zendesk Marketplace offers the ability to restrict installation to only certain roles and groups during installation, it is important to note that any buttons used for tickets assigned to groups or roles that are excluded from the installation will not function. To ensure that the buttons function properly for your tickets, please ensure that any role or group restrictions are selected in a manner such that tickets that use buttons are assigned to groups that are included in those installation restrictions. Zendesk Marketplace role/group restrictions function in such a manner that if both role and group restrictions are in place, then the agent that is assigned to the ticket must belong to BOTH the allowed group AND allowed role. \n \n \n Once the Marketplace installation process has completed, return to the Zendesk Support center by using the navigation drop-down and clicking on \"Support\": \n \n \n \n The Klick-Zen application icon should now be visible in the left-hand side menu within Zendesk; if it is not, simply refresh your browser screen. Click on this icon to begin the OAuth authentication process, which is used by Klick-Zen to operate within your Zendesk instance: \n \n \n \n The Klick-Zen authorization screen will appear; click \"Authorize\": \n \n \n \n The next screen completes the Zendesk OAuth process; click \"Allow\": \n \n For more details on managing the OAuth token that is created, please refer to the Zendesk documentation at https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/9447118302362-Managing-external-OAuth-clients \n \n \n Completely refresh your browser screen so that Klick-Zen may reload with the newly created OAuth token: \n \n \n \n Once the screen refresh has completed, Klick-Zen will display the main screen: \n \n \n \n Installation is now complete! You're now ready to create your first button ! \n \n \n \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "E1C-DLY-GS1-WUV", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/getting-started", "name": "Getting Started"}, {"url": "/en/getting-started/installation", "name": "Installation"}], "last_updated": "Aug 31, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Installation"], "pageName": "Installation", "pathName": "Getting-started > Installation", "parentName": "Getting Started", "relativePath": "/en/getting-started/installation"}, {"id": "U96-W0FS-NTH-J5V", "page": {"id": "U96-W0FS-NTH-J5V", "name": "Access Klick-Zen in Zendesk", "path": "/en/getting-started/access-klick-zen-in-zendesk", "slug": "access-klick-zen-in-zendesk", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "U96-W0FS-NTH-J5V", "slug": "access-klick-zen-in-zendesk", "title": "Access Klick-Zen in Zendesk", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 22:00:40", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/getting-started", "content": " Accessing Klick-Zen from Within Zendesk \n To begin using Klick-Zen after installation is complete, you can access the main user interface from the left-hand menu within Zendesk Support. Zendesk will be visible as an icon underneath the \"main\" Zendesk Support icons: \n \n If this icon is not visible within Zendesk, please review the installation instructions to ensure that Klick-Zen has been properly installed. If you are still experiencing issues, please contact support . \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "E1C-DLY-GS1-WUV", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/getting-started", "name": "Getting Started"}, {"url": "/en/getting-started/access-klick-zen-in-zendesk", "name": "Access Klick-Zen in Zendesk"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Accessing Klick-Zen from Within Zendesk"], "pageName": "Access Klick-Zen in Zendesk", "pathName": "Getting-started > Access-klick-zen-in-zendesk", "parentName": "Getting Started", "relativePath": "/en/getting-started/access-klick-zen-in-zendesk"}, {"id": "F14-9O9N-U25-0VB", "page": {"id": "F14-9O9N-U25-0VB", "name": "Quick Start", "path": "/en/getting-started/quick-start", "slug": "quick-start", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "F14-9O9N-U25-0VB", "slug": "quick-start", "title": "Quick Start", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:36:34", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/getting-started", "content": " Quick Start: Build Your First Button \n This walkthrough takes you from nothing to a working button in a few minutes. You will build a Close Ticket button that, when a customer clicks it, sets their ticket to Solved. By the end you will have the button created, styled, and ready to drop into a Zendesk email. \n This guide assumes Klick-Zen is already installed and authorized. If it isn't, see Getting Started first. \n \n Step 1: Open Klick-Zen and start a new button \n \n In Zendesk, open the Klick-Zen app from the left sidebar \n From the main left-hand menu, click Available KlickButtons \n \n Click the main part of the Create new button button \n (Create new button is a split button. Clicking the main part creates a blank button. Clicking the arrow on the right lets you start From a template… instead, which pre-fills a new button from a ready-made one.) \n \n \n \n \n You now have a blank button to configure: \n \n Change the button title where it currently reads \"Blank Button\" (these are not the words ON the button, these are the words that you use internally to reference the button): \n \n \n Step 2: Label the button \n In the button preview, type directly on the button to set its label. Enter: \n Close Ticket \n This is the text the customer will see, so keep it clear. \"Close Ticket\" tells them exactly what the click will do. (Labels can be up to 25 characters.) \n \n \n \n Step 3: Style it (optional) \n Your button already works with its default look, but since this is a positive, reassuring action, give it a friendly style: \n \n Set the Background to a green shade \n Set the Text color to white for contrast \n Set Bold on so the label stands out \n Adjust Roundness to taste \n \n \n See Button Appearance for the full set of customization options \n \n \n Step 4: Add the \"solve the ticket\" action \n This is the part that makes the button do something. Under Button Actions : \n \n \n Click the button \"Add button action\" \n \n \n \n In the action dropdown, choose Set Ticket Status \n \n \n \n For the status value, choose Solved \n \n \n \n That's the single action this button needs. When a customer clicks Close Ticket , their ticket will move to Solved. \n If your Zendesk validation rules require certain ticket fields to be populated before the ticket can be closed, you can set additional fields here to ensure that the validation requirements are met, such as setting an Assignee. If your normal Zendesk workflow already ensure that the fields are populated, then you can use the option \"If Blank\" for certain fields: \n \n \n Step 5: Save the button \n At the bottom of the page, click the Create button. Klick-Zen stores the button and generates its HTML snippet , which is how you place the button into Zendesk. Your Close Ticket button is now ready to use! \n \n \n Step 6: Put the button in an email \n Now place the button where a customer will see it. The quickest path is a Zendesk trigger that emails the customer: \n \n \n Back in Available KlickButtons , find your \"Close Ticket\" button and click the copy action to copy its HTML snippet: \n \n \n \n In Zendesk, open Admin Center → Objects and rules → Business rules → Triggers (or you can click on the link at the top of the page): \n \n \n \n Create or open a trigger that emails the requester (for example, a \"ticket updated\" notification) \n \n \n In the trigger's Email body , type your message, then paste the snippet where you want the button to appear \n \n \n \n Save the trigger \n \n \n \n You did it! \n You've built and placed your first Klick-Zen button! When the trigger sends its email, the customer sees a Close Ticket button, and a single click sets their ticket to Solved, with no reply and no login required. \n \n Where to go next \n \n Make it one-time , so a second click can't disturb a closed ticket: How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? \n Add a \"Still need help\" option beside it to give the customer a choice: How do I present the customer with a choice? \n Turn it into a full workflow by having the close fire a follow-up trigger, such as a CSAT request: How do I build a one-click ticket close? \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "E1C-DLY-GS1-WUV", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/getting-started", "name": "Getting Started"}, {"url": "/en/getting-started/quick-start", "name": "Quick Start"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Quick Start: Build Your First Button", "Step 1: Open Klick-Zen and start a new button", "Step 2: Label the button", "Step 3: Style it (optional)", "Step 4: Add the \"solve the ticket\" action", "Step 5: Save the button", "Step 6: Put the button in an email", "You did it!"], "pageName": "Quick Start", "pathName": "Getting-started > Quick-start", "parentName": "Getting Started", "relativePath": "/en/getting-started/quick-start"}, {"id": "G5L-RYO9-V1J-RDP", "page": {"id": "G5L-RYO9-V1J-RDP", "name": "Overview", "path": "/en/managing-buttons/overview", "slug": "overview", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "G5L-RYO9-V1J-RDP", "slug": "overview", "title": "Overview", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:12:46", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/managing-buttons", "content": " Klick-Zen Buttons \n Klick-Zen buttons are one-click action links, styled as clean, branded buttons, that you place directly inside your Zendesk emails and Help Center. Each button carries a set of ticket actions you define: update the status, add tags, insert a comment, assign the ticket, or change its priority or type. When a customer clicks, those actions are applied to their ticket instantly, with no login, no reply, and no trip through your support portal. \n Every button is rendered as an image hosted by Klick-Zen, so it displays consistently in every email client, and its look is fully yours to control, from text and font to colors and borders. A single button can perform several actions at once, and placing two or more side by side turns any email into a simple choice for the customer, such as \"Yes, close my ticket\" or \"I still need help.\" One click from the customer, and the ticket is updated, routed, and moving before they've even left their inbox. \n Your buttons are accessible from the \"Available KlickButtons\" menu within Klick-Zen: \n \n \n Finding your buttons: search, filter and sort \n A toolbar above the list lets you search, filter and sort, which is especially useful once you have many buttons: \n \n \n Search box. Matches on button names, button text, descriptions, labels, and the contents of actions and exceptions: field values, statuses, comment text, tags, assignee, type and priority. If a button matches on something its row doesn't normally show (for example, text inside a private comment), a Matched line appears on that row showing where the match was found. \n Label filter (\"Any label\"). Select one or more labels, or Unlabeled . Each option shows how many buttons it covers. This filter appears only once at least one button has a label. \n Action/exception filter (\"Any action or exception\"). Select one or more of: Update ticket field, Set ticket status, Add private comment, Add public comment, Add ticket tag, Set ticket assignee, Set ticket type, Set ticket priority, Skip if: tag exists, Skip if: status equals. \n Show dropdown: All buttons, Enabled, Disabled, In editor, Not in editor. \n Sort dropdown: Created: oldest (default), Created: newest, Name: A to Z, Name: Z to A. \n \n When any filter is active, the list shows \"Showing X of Y buttons · Clear filters\" . Clearing resets the search and filters but keeps the sort order. Your search, filters and sort are remembered in that browser and come back next time you open the app there; they are not shared with other admins. \n If you filter by In editor or Not in editor while editor KlickButtons are turned off in Settings, a notice reminds you that agents can't insert any buttons from the ticket editor, with a link to Settings. \n \n What each button row shows \n \n \n The button's preview image, name, and description. \n The button's labels , shown under its description. \n An Actions line listing what the button does when clicked. \n A Skip If line listing what exceptions will prevent a button click. \n A flag reading Not in ticket editor: no \"&lt;label&gt;\" label when the Ticket Editor Label setting limits the editor to one label and the button doesn't carry it. \n A link to copy the HTML snippet for insertion into triggers, automations, and macros \n \n \n Visit the links below for more details on... \n \n Creating a new button \n Customizing your buttons \n Setting actions for your buttons \n Editing, cloning, or deleting buttons \n Setting the base template for new buttons \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "88Z-T98O-4YG-EVO", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/managing-buttons", "name": "Managing Buttons"}, {"url": "/en/managing-buttons/overview", "name": "Overview"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Klick-Zen Buttons", "What each button row shows"], "pageName": "Overview", "pathName": "Managing-buttons > Overview", "parentName": "Managing Buttons", "relativePath": "/en/managing-buttons/overview"}, {"id": "VHJ-VULD-WJX-RUX", "page": {"id": "VHJ-VULD-WJX-RUX", "name": "Creating a New Button", "path": "/en/managing-buttons/creating-a-new-button", "slug": "creating-a-new-button", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "VHJ-VULD-WJX-RUX", "slug": "creating-a-new-button", "title": "Creating a New Button", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:11:24", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/managing-buttons", "content": " Creating a New Button \n A new button is created from the Available KlickButtons screen. Create new button is a split button: \n \n Click the main part to create a blank button and open the new-button form. \n \n Click the arrow on the right for a menu with two choices: Blank button and From a template… \n \n \n \n If you have not created any buttons, a few \"sample\" buttons that can get you up and running quickly will appear; simply click on one of the sample buttons to start with that sample as a template \n Choosing From a template… opens the Start from a template popup. Picking a template opens the new-button form pre-filled with that template's text, colors, actions and exceptions, and you can change anything before saving. \n A screen will then appear that allows you to set the details for your button: \n \n \n Naming Buttons \n When the \"new button\" screen is first visible, it is best practice to name your button. Although your customers never see this name, the name will be helpful when working with the buttons. Choose a name that is meaningful, but brief; there is an additional setting further down the page that will also allow you to include a more complete description for your internal use. \n \n If you wish to change this name within the button settings, simply click on the name itself while editing the button to enter edit mode. \n \n Actions and Exceptions \n The \"Actions\" and \"Exceptions\" sections are explained in more detail in the Actions and Exceptions portion of this documentation. \n \n Additional Options \n More options exist on this screen that relate to the overall function of the button: \n \n \n Button Description: internally used to provide more details on the usage of this button to your team \n Labels: optional internal tags for organizing your button list; agents and customers never see them. Pick an existing label or type a new one and press Enter. A button can have up to 10 labels , each up to 25 characters . Labels follow Zendesk tag rules (letters, numbers and underscores only, no spaces) and are not case-sensitive, so \"Billing\" and \"billing\" are the same label. Labels power the search and filters on the button list, and a label can be used to determine which buttons show in the ticket editor toolbar . \n Button Enabled: unchecking this box renders the button inactive; any button that still exists in a customer's email will appear \"greyed out\", and clicking the button will no longer affect any tickets \n Visible in Ticket Editor: use this option to show/hide this button to your Zendesk agents in the Ticket Editor Toolbar (note: this option is used in concert with the main settings option that shows buttons in the editor based on a label; if the label option is used, this checkbox must also be checked for the button to show in the ticket editor) \n Post-Action URL: allows you to provide a specific URL for this button to show instead of the built-in Confirmation Page \n \n \n Finalize the Button \n After adding at least one button action , you may then create the button by clicking \"Create\". \n \n \n Starter templates \n Starter templates are ready-made buttons you can use as a starting point. Open them from the From a template… menu on the Create new button split button; select a template button by simply clicking on it: \n \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "88Z-T98O-4YG-EVO", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/managing-buttons", "name": "Managing Buttons"}, {"url": "/en/managing-buttons/creating-a-new-button", "name": "Creating a New Button"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Creating a New Button", "Naming Buttons", "Actions and Exceptions", "Additional Options", "Finalize the Button", "Starter templates"], "pageName": "Creating a New Button", "pathName": "Managing-buttons > Creating-a-new-button", "parentName": "Managing Buttons", "relativePath": "/en/managing-buttons/creating-a-new-button"}, {"id": "JTE-VKFM-BHD-PQ0", "page": {"id": "JTE-VKFM-BHD-PQ0", "name": "Button Appearance", "path": "/en/managing-buttons/button-appearance", "slug": "button-appearance", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "JTE-VKFM-BHD-PQ0", "slug": "button-appearance", "title": "Button Appearance", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-09-09 19:27:27", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/managing-buttons", "content": " Button Appearance \n This section controls everything your customer sees: the label, the typography, the colors, and the shape. A live preview sits at the top and updates as you work, so what you see here is exactly what will render in the email. \n \n Button Text \n To change the label, type directly inside the button preview itself . Labels are limited to 25 characters. Keep them short and action oriented, such as \"Yes, close my ticket\" or \"I still need help.\" \n Text Styling \n \n Bold : switches the label between regular and bold weight \n Italic : renders the label in italics \n Font : choose the typeface from the dropdown; because the button is rendered as a hosted image, the font displays exactly as chosen in every email client \n \n Colors \n Click any swatch to open the color picker: \n \n Border : the color of the button's outline \n Background : the fill color of the button body \n Text : the color of the label; choose a color with strong contrast against the background \n \n The circle with two arrows next to the border color allow you to quickly \"erase\" the button border by matching the border color to the background color: \n \n Roundness \n The Roundness slider controls the corner curvature, from sharp squared corners on the left to soft pill-shaped edges on the right. \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "88Z-T98O-4YG-EVO", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/managing-buttons", "name": "Managing Buttons"}, {"url": "/en/managing-buttons/button-appearance", "name": "Button Appearance"}], "last_updated": "Sep 9, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Button Appearance"], "pageName": "Button Appearance", "pathName": "Managing-buttons > Button-appearance", "parentName": "Managing Buttons", "relativePath": "/en/managing-buttons/button-appearance"}, {"id": "FQH-7QP3-7TU-UHX", "page": {"id": "FQH-7QP3-7TU-UHX", "name": "Actions and Exceptions", "path": "/en/managing-buttons/button-actions", "slug": "button-actions", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "FQH-7QP3-7TU-UHX", "slug": "button-actions", "title": "Actions and Exceptions", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:45:11", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/managing-buttons", "content": " Actions and Exceptions \n Actions are what a click does to the ticket, and exceptions are the conditions under which it should do nothing instead. Together they define the button's behavior: every button needs at least one action, and exceptions are optional guardrails on top of them. \n \n Working with Actions \n To add an action, click the \"Add button action\" button: \n \n Each button MUST have at least one action defined before you can save any changes to your button \n When you click the button to add an action, you must first choose which action to take: \n \n Depending on the action selected, you will have further details that must be added to the action. Some of the actions require special attention when setting their values: \n Action Watch out for... Update Ticket Field Note that the primary Zendesk ticket fields (Status, Type, etc) have their own settings available in the drop-down. Zendesk may require that the new value for the field matches a TAG value, and not the DISPLAY value; see the following Zendesk article for more information: https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/6554073287706-Using-User-Organization-Tags-to-Set-Drop-Down-Ticket-Fields Set Ticket Status Zendesk built-in rules may require that certain fields are set before the ticket status may be set to \"Solved\"; if these fields are not also set, then this action will fail Add Private/Public Comment The identity of the user that will have appeared to make comments that are automatically inserted into tickets defaults to that of the user who installed and configured Klick-Zen; this identity may be changed in the Klick-Zen settings Set Ticket Assignee To assign a specific Zendesk agent to a ticket, enter their email address. There is also an option available for this to only be set if the ticket is not already assigned to an agent (\"If Blank\") Set Ticket Type An option is available to only set the ticket type if it has not already been set (\"If Blank\") Set Ticket Priority An option is available to only set the ticket priority if it has not already been set (\"If Blank\") \n \n Tips for working with Actions: \n \n \n Once you have selected the button action type, that type can no longer be changed for that action; the action must be removed and re-added \n \n \n You may have a maximum of six actions (and six exceptions) per button \n \n \n To remove an action, hover your mouse over the action rule and then click the \"X\" that appears at the end of the row: \n \n \n \n Some actions (such as \"Set Ticket Type\" and \"Add Private Comment\") may only be added once per button \n \n \n Button actions can also be connected to Zendesk triggers for greater flexibility and functionality (such as sending emails, firing API calls, etc) \n \n \n \n Working With Exceptions \n To add an exception, click the \"Add button exception\" button: \n \n When you click the button to add an exception, you must choose which exception type to use: \n \n \n Tag Exists : if the tag that is stored with this exception exists on the ticket, then the button will not take any actions \n Status Equals : if the ticket has this status, then the button will not take any actions; more than one status exception may be added to the button exceptions, effectively enabling you to string them together (in other words, prevent the button from taking any action unless the status is \"Pending\", by simply adding all other status options to the exception list BESIDES \"Pending\") \n \n To remove an exception, hover your mouse over the exception rule and then click the \"X\" that appears at the end of the row: \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "88Z-T98O-4YG-EVO", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/managing-buttons", "name": "Managing Buttons"}, {"url": "/en/managing-buttons/button-actions", "name": "Actions and Exceptions"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Actions and Exceptions", "Working with Actions", "Working With Exceptions"], "pageName": "Actions and Exceptions", "pathName": "Managing-buttons > Button-actions", "parentName": "Managing Buttons", "relativePath": "/en/managing-buttons/button-actions"}, {"id": "9T4-4RAW-OC8-W3U", "page": {"id": "9T4-4RAW-OC8-W3U", "name": "Edit, Clone, Delete", "path": "/en/managing-buttons/edit-clone-delete", "slug": "edit-clone-delete", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "9T4-4RAW-OC8-W3U", "slug": "edit-clone-delete", "title": "Edit, Clone, Delete", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 22:03:08", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/managing-buttons", "content": " Edit, Clone, and Delete \n Buttons aren't set in stone. Open any existing button to adjust its appearance or actions, clone one to use as a starting point for a similar button, or delete one you no longer need. \n Editing, cloning, and deleting a button can all be performed from the main \"Available KlickButtons\" screen: \n \n When a button is changed in any way (either visually or by way of what actions/exceptions it takes), the changes apply to all instances of that button immediately . Any clicks of that button (in an email or otherwise) will execute the new actions for that button, not the actions that were in place when the button was first delivered to their email. \n \n Editing a Button \n To edit a button, simply hover your mouse over the button row, then click on the \"Edit\" button visible directly underneath the name of the desired button; you will be taken directly to the button editing screen: \n \n \n Cloning a Button \n Button cloning is useful when you wish to create multiple buttons that perform similar tasks, or perhaps two separate but similar buttons that direct the users to two different landing pages. To clone a button, hover your mouse over the button row, then click on the \"Clone\" button visible directly underneath the name of the desired button. A new button will be created, and you will be taken directly to the edit screen for the new button. \n \n \n Deleting a Button \n Delete a button if you are certain that you are no longer going to be making use of that button. To delete the button, hover your mouse over the entire button row, and click on the \"X\" that appears on the right-hand side of the row: \n \n When a button is deleted, it will no longer render within an end-users email. Only delete a button when you are certain it is no longer in use. If you wish to disable the use of a button, but would prefer that it does not appear \"broken\" in existing emails, you may choose to disable the button first so that it appears \"greyed-out\" to the end user. For more details on this option, see Creating a New Button . \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "88Z-T98O-4YG-EVO", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/managing-buttons", "name": "Managing Buttons"}, {"url": "/en/managing-buttons/edit-clone-delete", "name": "Edit, Clone, Delete"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Edit, Clone, and Delete", "Editing a Button", "Cloning a Button", "Deleting a Button"], "pageName": "Edit, Clone, Delete", "pathName": "Managing-buttons > Edit-clone-delete", "parentName": "Managing Buttons", "relativePath": "/en/managing-buttons/edit-clone-delete"}, {"id": "R9U-EYC2-M3H-RXW", "page": {"id": "R9U-EYC2-M3H-RXW", "name": "Default Button Template", "path": "/en/managing-buttons/default-button-template", "slug": "default-button-template", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "R9U-EYC2-M3H-RXW", "slug": "default-button-template", "title": "Default Button Template", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 22:05:02", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/managing-buttons", "content": " Default Button Template \n The default button template sets the starting point for every new button you create. Configure your brand's colors, font, and styling once here, and new buttons will begin already on brand instead of requiring the same adjustments each time. \n To access the Default Button Template, click the corresponding link in the main left-hand menu: \n \n The default button template screen contains basic options that will be used for new buttons that are created: \n \n \n If you wish to reset the template back to the Klick-Zen default settings, click the \"Reset\" button: \n \n \n The default button template only affects newly-created buttons going forward; changing this template has no effect on existing buttons \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "88Z-T98O-4YG-EVO", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/managing-buttons", "name": "Managing Buttons"}, {"url": "/en/managing-buttons/default-button-template", "name": "Default Button Template"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Default Button Template"], "pageName": "Default Button Template", "pathName": "Managing-buttons > Default-button-template", "parentName": "Managing Buttons", "relativePath": "/en/managing-buttons/default-button-template"}, {"id": "LV0-RJA3-21Y-V5U", "page": {"id": "LV0-RJA3-21Y-V5U", "name": "Overview", "path": "/en/landing-pages/overview", "slug": "overview", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "LV0-RJA3-21Y-V5U", "slug": "overview", "title": "Overview", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 22:05:49", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/landing-pages", "content": " Landing Pages \n Clicking a button in an email doesn't update the ticket directly from within the user's email inbox. Instead, the button's link points to a URL and clicking any link in an email opens a web browser to load it, which is exactly how Klick-Zen applies the ticket actions: the browser's request to that URL is what triggers the update on the backend. The landing page is simply what the browser displays once that request completes, giving the customer visible confirmation that their click did something rather than leaving them on a blank tab or an error. \n You can modify the landing pages by clicking on \"Default Landing Page\" in the main left-hand menu: \n \n By default, Klick-Zen hosts this confirmation page for you, customized with your colors, logo, and message, so there's nothing to build or host yourself. \n Landing pages are relatively simple, and are designed to provide a clear message on both mobile and desktop devices. For a more feature-rich user experience, such as a knowledge base article, a support page, or a survey, you also have the option of creating and hosting your own landing page . \n \n There are two types of customizable Klick-Zen-hosted landing pages available; visit the links below for more details on... \n \n the \"confirmation\" landing page \n the \"ticket closed\" landing page \n \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "QZ5-CR0F-15N-WK6", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/landing-pages", "name": "Landing Pages"}, {"url": "/en/landing-pages/overview", "name": "Overview"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Landing Pages"], "pageName": "Overview", "pathName": "Landing-pages > Overview", "parentName": "Landing Pages", "relativePath": "/en/landing-pages/overview"}, {"id": "QXS-FO3I-N65-Q22", "page": {"id": "QXS-FO3I-N65-Q22", "name": "Landing Page Settings", "path": "/en/landing-pages/landing-page-settings", "slug": "landing-page-settings", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "QXS-FO3I-N65-Q22", "slug": "landing-page-settings", "title": "Landing Page Settings", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-07-20 19:01:10", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/landing-pages", "content": " Global Landing Page Settings \n Global landing page settings are the \"aesthetics\" of the landing pages; this is where the general look and feel of both the \"confirmation\" page and the \"ticket closed\" page are defined: \n \n Since the actual text of the message in these pages are defined by simple markdown , these settings offer an opportunity to apply a custom style to the landing page. This presents a look and feel to the page that is consistent with your branding (such as your company's logo and standard color scheme), and when combined with a custom subdomain , offers a seamless experience to your customers. \n \n HTML Browser Title \n This is the text that is shown in the browser tab; this typically includes your company name, but can include any text that you feel is appropriate: \n \n Template Colors \n Typically set to match your corporate color scheme, the following color themed items may be set: \n \n Brand Color: This color is used for bold outlines, and is usually the darker of your scheme colors \n Accent Color: This color is used for the text background, and is usually the lighter of your scheme colors \n Text Color: This color is used for the text content of each page \n \n Since the \"accent color\" is used for the text background, be sure to select a color that offers good readability with your chosen \"text color\" \n Page Header Image \n This image is displayed center-aligned at the top of the page, and is often a company logo. The image should be a typical web image file such as JPG, GIF, or PNG, and should not exceed 3 megabytes in size. To alter the header image, click the \"select new image\" button, and browse to the desired file: \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "QZ5-CR0F-15N-WK6", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/landing-pages", "name": "Landing Pages"}, {"url": "/en/landing-pages/landing-page-settings", "name": "Landing Page Settings"}], "last_updated": "Jul 20, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Global Landing Page Settings"], "pageName": "Landing Page Settings", "pathName": "Landing-pages > Landing-page-settings", "parentName": "Landing Pages", "relativePath": "/en/landing-pages/landing-page-settings"}, {"id": "XAZ-J83T-8YD-MEQ", "page": {"id": "XAZ-J83T-8YD-MEQ", "name": "\"Confirmation\" Page", "path": "/en/landing-pages/confirmation-page", "slug": "confirmation-page", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "XAZ-J83T-8YD-MEQ", "slug": "confirmation-page", "title": "\"Confirmation\" Page", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 22:06:51", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/landing-pages", "content": " \"Confirmation\" Landing Page \n The confirmation page is displayed when the underlying ticket is active, and is useful for confirming to the end user that their response has been processed. \n \n The left-hand side of this section is the \"edit\" area; use this side to enter the text that you wish to display. This text is entered in markdown syntax, which allows for basic formatting (such as making text bold, and adding self-hosted images). The right-hand side of this section is the \"preview\" area, and will show a rough preview of how the final rendered text will display to your end user. \n The edit area has a toolbar at the top which can automatically add markdown syntax for common formatting, such as italicization and bulleted lists. You may also enter markdown syntax manually in this area; for a more complete overview of markdown syntax, please see the online markdown syntax guide at https://markdownguide.offshoot.io/basic-syntax . \n For security, the content of the message is sanitized to disable any active content from being rendered within the final displayed page, such as HTML, JavaScript, and CSS \n To save your changes, click on the \"Update\" button at the bottom of the page: \n \n \n Previewing the Page \n Once your changes have been saved, you can view a live preview of the final page rendering by clicking the \"preview this page\" button: \n \n The \"preview this page\" button is disabled until any changes to the pages have been saved by clicking the \"Update\" button at the bottom of the page \n \n \n Example of a \"Confirmation\" Landing Page: \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "QZ5-CR0F-15N-WK6", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/landing-pages", "name": "Landing Pages"}, {"url": "/en/landing-pages/confirmation-page", "name": "\"Confirmation\" Page"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["\"Confirmation\" Landing Page", "Previewing the Page", "Example of a \"Confirmation\" Landing Page:", "\n"], "pageName": "\"Confirmation\" Page", "pathName": "Landing-pages > Confirmation-page", "parentName": "Landing Pages", "relativePath": "/en/landing-pages/confirmation-page"}, {"id": "GOE-P4KH-VTB-H62", "page": {"id": "GOE-P4KH-VTB-H62", "name": "\"Ticket Closed\" Page", "path": "/en/landing-pages/ticket-closed-page", "slug": "ticket-closed-page", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "GOE-P4KH-VTB-H62", "slug": "ticket-closed-page", "title": "\"Ticket Closed\" Page", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-23 22:07:32", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/landing-pages", "content": " \"Ticket Closed\" Landing Page \n The ticket closed page is displayed when the underlying ticket is no longer active, such as when the ticket has already been solved and closed, and is no longer accepting changes. This gives the end user helpful feedback that their response is no longer able to be handled. \n \n The left-hand side of this section is the \"edit\" area; use this side to enter the text that you wish to display. This text is entered in markdown syntax, which allows for basic formatting (such as making text bold, and adding self-hosted images). The right-hand side of this section is the \"preview\" area, and will show a rough preview of how the final rendered text will display to your end user. \n The edit area has a toolbar at the top which can automatically add markdown syntax for common formatting, such as italicization and bulleted lists. You may also enter markdown syntax manually in this area; for a more complete overview of markdown syntax, please see the online markdown syntax guide at https://markdownguide.offshoot.io/basic-syntax . \n For security, the content of the message is sanitized to disable any active content from being rendered within the final displayed page, such as HTML, JavaScript, and CSS \n To save your changes, click on the \"Update\" button at the bottom of the page: \n \n \n Previewing the Page \n Once your changes have been saved, you can view a live preview of the final page rendering by clicking the \"preview this page\" button: \n \n The \"preview this page\" button is disabled until any changes to the pages have been saved by clicking the \"Update\" button at the bottom of the page \n \n \n Example of a \"Ticket Closed\" Landing Page: \n \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "QZ5-CR0F-15N-WK6", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/landing-pages", "name": "Landing Pages"}, {"url": "/en/landing-pages/ticket-closed-page", "name": "\"Ticket Closed\" Page"}], "last_updated": "Oct 23, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["\"Ticket Closed\" Landing Page", "Previewing the Page", "Example of a \"Ticket Closed\" Landing Page:"], "pageName": "\"Ticket Closed\" Page", "pathName": "Landing-pages > Ticket-closed-page", "parentName": "Landing Pages", "relativePath": "/en/landing-pages/ticket-closed-page"}, {"id": "2TS-AVZJ-XNT-CQQ", "page": {"id": "2TS-AVZJ-XNT-CQQ", "name": "Overview", "path": "/en/ticket-editor-toolbar/overview", "slug": "overview", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "2TS-AVZJ-XNT-CQQ", "slug": "overview", "title": "Overview", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:37:47", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/ticket-editor-toolbar", "content": " Ticket Editor Toolbar \n The Klick-Zen toolbar adds a button icon directly to the Zendesk ticket editor, letting agents insert any button into a reply while typing it. This is useful for one-off situations an automation wouldn't catch: offering a customer a quick way to confirm a fix worked, gathering additional information, or giving them a choice of next steps, all without leaving the ticket. \n \n Enabling the Toolbar \n The toolbar can be enabled within the Settings section (accessible from the left-hand main menu): \n \n The toolbar is enabled by default; if you do not wish for your agents to use the toolbar, it must be disabled in the settings \n \n Accessing the Toolbar \n The Klick-Zen toolbar icon is automatically added to the ticket editor toolbar when Klick-Zen is installed; it can be accessed by a Zendesk agent when typing a reply within the Zendesk ticket editor. \n \n \n Individual Button Visibility in the Toolbar \n If you only wish for certain buttons to be visible within the toolbar, the undesired buttons can be hidden directly from within the individual button settings : \n \n You can also restrict the toolbar to a single label. In Settings, the Ticket Editor Label option limits the toolbar pop-up to buttons carrying a chosen label. If the pop-up shows fewer buttons than expected, or none at all, check that setting. \n \n \n Using the Toolbar \n For more details on how to use the toolbar within the Zendesk ticket editor, please see \"Ticket Editor\" in \"Using Buttons within Zendesk\" . \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "M0N-0PWL-MO4-TG7", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/ticket-editor-toolbar", "name": "Ticket Editor Toolbar"}, {"url": "/en/ticket-editor-toolbar/overview", "name": "Overview"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Ticket Editor Toolbar"], "pageName": "Overview", "pathName": "Ticket-editor-toolbar > Overview", "parentName": "Ticket Editor Toolbar", "relativePath": "/en/ticket-editor-toolbar/overview"}, {"id": "ATT-EXHN-26S-0QP", "page": {"id": "ATT-EXHN-26S-0QP", "name": "Ticket Editor", "path": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/tickets", "slug": "tickets", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "ATT-EXHN-26S-0QP", "slug": "tickets", "title": "Ticket Editor", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:42:59", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "content": " Using Buttons in the Ticket Editor \n The Klick-Zen toolbar adds a button icon directly to the Zendesk ticket editor, letting agents drop any button into a reply while composing it. This is useful for one-off situations an automation wouldn't catch: offering a customer a quick way to confirm a fix worked, gathering additional information, or giving them a choice of next steps, all without leaving the ticket. \n \n Inserting a Button \n \n \n While in an active ticket within Zendesk Support, compose your reply in the Zendesk ticket editor as usual \n \n \n Place your cursor where you want the button to appear \n \n \n Click the Klick-Zen icon in the editor toolbar; a pop-up opens showing the buttons available in your account: \n \n \n \n Click the button you want. Each entry in the pop-up is displayed as a small image of the button itself, so you're picking by appearance rather than by name. \n \n \n The button is inserted at your cursor position and rendered visibly in the message area, exactly as it will appear in the customer's email. Continue typing around it, or send the reply as usual \n \n Requirements and Limitations \n A few conditions govern when the toolbar is available and which buttons appear in it: \n \n \n The rich text editor is required. The Klick-Zen toolbar icon is only available in Zendesk's rich text editor. It does not appear when composing in plain text mode. \n \n \n The ticket must be created and saved. Buttons cannot be inserted into a reply on a newly created ticket that hasn't been saved yet; if you attempt to do this, you will see the message like this: \n \n Because ad-hoc buttons resolve their values at insertion time, those values have to exist first, and on an unsaved ticket they don't. Save the ticket, then insert the button. \n \n \n Buttons can be hidden from the toolbar. Individual buttons can be excluded from the toolbar pop-up on a per-button basis in that button's settings. If a button you expect to see is missing from the pop-up, check whether it has been hidden in the individual button settings. A label can restrict the toolbar. An admin can set the Ticket Editor Label option in Settings so the pop-up shows only buttons carrying a chosen label. If the pop-up shows fewer buttons than expected, or none at all, check that setting. \n \n \n \n Inserting Multiple Buttons \n You can insert more than one button into a single reply. Position the cursor at each spot where you want a button and repeat the steps above. In this manner, you can present the customer with a choice, such as a \"Resolved\" button alongside a \"Still need help\" button. \n \n How Ad-hoc Buttons Differ from Template Buttons \n When a button is placed in a trigger, automation, or macro, its link carries Zendesk placeholders that are resolved later, at send time. Buttons inserted from the ticket editor work differently: because the agent is replying to a specific ticket, all of the relevant values are already known. Klick-Zen inserts the actual values in place of the placeholders at the moment of insertion, so the link is fully resolved as soon as it appears in the editor. \n Be very careful to not copy-paste an ad-hoc button into any other ticket message or email; buttons inserted via the ticket editor are hard-coded to affect only the ticket that they were originally inserted into \n \n Changing the Button from an Image to a Text Link \n If you wish to use a text link in the ticket editor (instead of a button image), this can be accomplished since the ad-hoc link contains actual values in place of the placeholders. To insert this type of link: \n \n Insert the desired button per the steps above . \n Right-click on the button image and select \"Copy Link\" from the pop-up menu. IMPORTANT: this is not the same as the template link that you can copy from the main Klick-Zen screen (which contains Zendesk ticket placeholders); when you copy this link, it will contain the ticket values that are unique to the ticket you are working in, and can be used ONLY for that ticket. \n \n Using a keyboard shortcut or right-click, paste the recently-copied link into the email message. You may also use the ticket editor toolbar's \"link\" function to attach the link to text that is within your message. \n Selecting the entire button allows you to edit or copy the ticket-specific link by using the Zendesk ticket editor \"add link\" tool \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "DTS-918J-0F3-G0S", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "name": "Using Buttons within Zendesk"}, {"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/tickets", "name": "Ticket Editor"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Using Buttons in the Ticket Editor", "Inserting a Button", "Requirements and Limitations", "Inserting Multiple Buttons", "How Ad-hoc Buttons Differ from Template Buttons", "Changing the Button from an Image to a Text Link"], "pageName": "Ticket Editor", "pathName": "Using-buttons-within-zendesk > Tickets", "parentName": "Using Buttons within Zendesk", "relativePath": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/tickets"}, {"id": "H8F-3CLQ-KZE-DM6", "page": {"id": "H8F-3CLQ-KZE-DM6", "name": "Triggers", "path": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/triggers", "slug": "triggers", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "H8F-3CLQ-KZE-DM6", "slug": "triggers", "title": "Triggers", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-13 20:32:15", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "content": " Using Buttons in Triggers \n A Zendesk trigger runs the moment a ticket is created or updated and meets the conditions you set. One of the actions a trigger can perform is sending an email notification, and the body of that notification accepts HTML. Because every Klick-Zen button is delivered as an HTML snippet, you can simply paste the snippet into the trigger's email body. The snippet is self-contained; it already carries the button's action and its association to the ticket (via Zendesk placeholders), so it works unchanged in an automated email, and each recipient receives a button wired to their own ticket. \n Zendesk officially supports HTML in triggers. See Where can I use Markdown and HTML in Zendesk Support? \n \n Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen \n \n Open the Klick-Zen app from the left sidebar in Zendesk \n From the main left-hand menu, click Available KlickButtons \n Ensure that the toggle option \"Help Center Links\" is NOT toggled \"on\" for this operation \n \n Find the button you want to use and click the copy action on the right-hand side of the HTML snipper; The button's HTML snippet is copied to your clipboard: \n \n Keep the snippet on your clipboard for Step 4 below \n \n \n Ensure that the toggle option \"Help Center Links\" (visible in the screenshot above) is NOT toggled \"on\" for this operation \n \n Step 2: Create or open a Zendesk trigger \n \n In Zendesk, open Admin Center \n Go to Objects and rules → Business rules → Triggers \n Click Add trigger to create a new one (or open an existing trigger you want to add the button to) \n Give the trigger a clear name , and optionally assign it to a category \n \n \n Step 3: Set the trigger conditions \n Under Conditions , define exactly when the email should be sent. Add conditions under Meet ALL of the following conditions (and/or Meet ANY ) so the trigger only fires for the tickets you intend. \n Common conditions for a customer-facing button email include the ticket Status , Channel , Tags , or a specific ticket field value. \n \n Step 4: Add the email action and paste the snippet \n \n Under Actions , add the action Notify by → User email \n Choose the recipient (typically Ticket → requester ) so that the customer receives the button \n Fill in the Email subject \n \n In the Email body , type any message you want the customer to read, then place your cursor where the button should appear and paste the Klick-Zen snippet you copied in Step 1 \n \n \n \n If you notice extra gaps or blank space around the button in the delivered email, make sure the snippet is on a single line (minified). Zendesk recommends minified, one-line HTML in trigger bodies. See Where can I use Markdown and HTML in Zendesk Support? Klick-Zen snippets are already single-line, so simply avoid adding line breaks inside the pasted code. \n Tips \n \n Modify the button wrapper HTML in any manner that suits your use case; for example, you can use plain text as the hyperlink instead of an image, or wrap the hyperlink around your own hosted image \n Add more than one button to give the customer a choice (for example, \"Resolved\" and \"Still need help\"); create each button in Klick-Zen and paste both snippets into the same email body \n Chain actions with a follow-up trigger by having your button add a specific tag to a ticket when clicked, and then having another trigger fire when the tag is added to the ticket by the button click; use this to route, reassign, or send a \"thanks for confirming\" reply automatically after the customer clicks \n \n Troubleshooting \n \n The button shows up as raw code or plain text: This usually means the content was entered somewhere that doesn't render HTML. Confirm you pasted the snippet into a trigger (or automation) email body (HTML is supported there) and not into a plain-text-only field \n The button image doesn't appear: Some email clients block remote images until the recipient allows them. Klick-Zen hosts the button images; once the recipient loads images, the button displays normally \n Extra spacing around the button: Keep the snippet on a single line and avoid inserting line breaks inside it \n \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "DTS-918J-0F3-G0S", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "name": "Using Buttons within Zendesk"}, {"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/triggers", "name": "Triggers"}], "last_updated": "Oct 13, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Using Buttons in Triggers", "Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen", "Step 2: Create or open a Zendesk trigger", "Step 3: Set the trigger conditions", "Step 4: Add the email action and paste the snippet", "Tips", "Troubleshooting"], "pageName": "Triggers", "pathName": "Using-buttons-within-zendesk > Triggers", "parentName": "Using Buttons within Zendesk", "relativePath": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/triggers"}, {"id": "27Y-2VSI-74M-Z5G", "page": {"id": "27Y-2VSI-74M-Z5G", "name": "Automations", "path": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/automations", "slug": "automations", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "27Y-2VSI-74M-Z5G", "slug": "automations", "title": "Automations", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-13 20:32:26", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "content": " Using Buttons in Automations \n A Zendesk automation runs on a schedule rather than in response to a single event. Zendesk evaluates automations once per hour and performs their actions on every ticket that meets the conditions you set. One of the actions an automation can perform is sending an email notification, and the body of that notification accepts HTML. Because every Klick-Zen button is delivered as an HTML snippet, you can simply paste the snippet into the automation's email body. The snippet is self-contained; it already carries the button's action and its association to the ticket (via Zendesk placeholders), so it works unchanged in an automated email, and each recipient receives a button wired to their own ticket. \n Automations are ideal for time-based button emails: a reminder sent 24 hours after a ticket goes pending, a follow-up a few days after a ticket is solved, or a nudge on tickets that have sat without a customer reply. In each case, the email carries a Klick-Zen button the customer can click to update their own ticket. \n Zendesk officially supports HTML in automations. See Where can I use Markdown and HTML in Zendesk Support? \n Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen \n \n Open the Klick-Zen app from the left sidebar in Zendesk \n From the main left-hand menu, click Available KlickButtons \n Ensure that the toggle option \"Help Center Links\" is NOT toggled \"on\" for this operation \n \n Find the button you want to use and click the copy action on the right-hand side of the HTML snippet; The button's HTML snippet is copied to your clipboard: \n \n Keep the snippet on your clipboard for Step 4 below \n \n \n Ensure that the toggle option \"Help Center Links\" (visible in the screenshot above) is NOT toggled \"on\" for this operation \n \n Step 2: Create or open a Zendesk automation \n \n In Zendesk, open Admin Center \n Go to Objects and rules → Business rules → Automations \n Click Add automation to create a new one (or open an existing automation you want to add the button to) \n Give the automation a clear name \n \n \n Step 3: Set the automation conditions \n Under Conditions , define exactly when the email should be sent. Add conditions under Meet ALL of the following conditions (and/or Meet ANY ) so the automation only acts on the tickets you intend. \n Unlike a trigger, an automation must include at least one time-based condition , such as Hours since created , Hours since pending , or Hours since solved , is greater than a value you specify. This is what makes automations suited to delayed or scheduled button emails. \n Because an automation runs every hour, you must give it a condition it can no longer meet after it acts; otherwise it will send the same email hour after hour. The standard approach is to have the automation add a tag as one of its actions, then add a condition that the ticket does not already carry that tag. Once the automation runs and applies the tag, the ticket stops matching, and the email is sent only once. \n Common conditions for a customer-facing button email include a time-based condition (required), plus the ticket Status , Channel , Tags , or a specific ticket field value. \n \n Step 4: Add the email action and paste the snippet \n \n \n Under Actions , add the action Notifications: User email \n \n \n Choose the recipient (typically requester ) so that the customer receives the button \n \n \n Fill in the Email subject \n \n \n In the Email body , type any message you want the customer to read, then place your cursor where the button should appear and paste the Klick-Zen snippet you copied in Step 1 \n \n \n \n If you are using a tag to stop the automation from repeating (see the warning in Step 3), also add the action Add tags with that tag here \n \n \n If you notice extra gaps or blank space around the button in the delivered email, make sure the snippet is on a single line (minified). Zendesk recommends minified, one-line HTML in trigger bodies. See Where can I use Markdown and HTML in Zendesk Support? Klick-Zen snippets are already single-line, so simply avoid adding line breaks inside the pasted code. \n \n Tips \n \n Modify the button wrapper HTML in any manner that suits your use case; for example, you can use plain text as the hyperlink instead of an image, or wrap the hyperlink around your own hosted image \n Add more than one button to give the customer a choice (for example, \"Resolved\" and \"Still need help\"); create each button in Klick-Zen and paste both snippets into the same email body \n Use automations for escalating reminders by creating several automations at increasing time thresholds (for example, 24, 48, and 72 hours), each sending a button email; tag the ticket at each stage so the next automation can tell which reminders have already gone out \n \n Troubleshooting \n \n The email sends repeatedly, every hour: The automation has no condition that stops it from matching after it runs. Add a tag as an action, and add a condition that the ticket does not already have that tag, so the automation acts only once per ticket \n The automation never sends: Automations require at least one time-based condition and only run once per hour, so allow up to an hour for it to fire. Also confirm the ticket actually meets every condition under Meet ALL \n The button shows up as raw code or plain text: This usually means the content was entered somewhere that doesn't render HTML. Confirm you pasted the snippet into an automation email body (HTML is supported there) and not into a plain-text-only field \n The button image doesn't appear: Some email clients block remote images until the recipient allows them. Klick-Zen hosts the button images; once the recipient loads images, the button displays normally \n Extra spacing around the button: Keep the snippet on a single line and avoid inserting line breaks inside it \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "DTS-918J-0F3-G0S", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "name": "Using Buttons within Zendesk"}, {"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/automations", "name": "Automations"}], "last_updated": "Oct 13, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Using Buttons in Automations", "Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen", "Step 2: Create or open a Zendesk automation", "Step 3: Set the automation conditions", "Step 4: Add the email action and paste the snippet", "Tips", "Troubleshooting"], "pageName": "Automations", "pathName": "Using-buttons-within-zendesk > Automations", "parentName": "Using Buttons within Zendesk", "relativePath": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/automations"}, {"id": "YE0-CA4O-S3K-TYH", "page": {"id": "YE0-CA4O-S3K-TYH", "name": "Macros", "path": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/macros", "slug": "macros", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "YE0-CA4O-S3K-TYH", "slug": "macros", "title": "Macros", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-13 20:32:32", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "content": " Using Buttons in Macros \n A Zendesk macro is a reusable response an agent applies to a ticket with a single click. Macros are ideal for the questions and situations your team handles over and over; adding a Klick-Zen button to a macro means an agent can send a consistent, action-ready reply without pasting the button by hand each time. \n Unlike triggers and automations, which send email automatically, a macro is applied by an agent. When the agent applies the macro, its comment is placed into the reply, and the button goes out with the response. This makes macros a good fit for common but agent-initiated moments, such as asking a customer to confirm a fix, offering a choice of next steps, or gathering one more piece of information. \n \n Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen \n \n Open the Klick-Zen app from the left sidebar in Zendesk \n From the main left-hand menu, click Available KlickButtons \n Ensure that the toggle option \"Help Center Links\" is NOT toggled \"on\" for this operation \n \n Find the button you want to use and click the copy action on the right-hand side of the HTML snippet; The button's HTML snippet is copied to your clipboard: \n \n Keep the snippet on your clipboard for Step 3 below \n \n \n Ensure that the toggle option \"Help Center Links\" (visible in the screenshot above) is NOT toggled \"on\" for this operation \n \n Step 2: Create or open a Zendesk macro \n \n In Zendesk, open Admin Center \n Go to Workspaces → Agent tools → Macros \n Click Create macro to create a new one (or open an existing macro you want to add the button to) \n Give the macro a clear name so agents can find it easily \n \n \n Step 3: Add the comment action and paste the snippet \n \n \n Under Actions , add the action Comment/description \n \n \n In the comment editor, type any message you want included in the reply, then place your cursor where the button should appear and paste the Klick-Zen snippet you copied in Step 1. The macro comment editor is a rich text editor , so the pasted snippet displays as the actual button image (with its link already embedded) \n \n \n \n Save the macro \n \n \n Because the comment editor renders the button as an image, you can position it, add text around it, and preview the layout exactly as the customer will see it, all before saving the macro. \n \n Applying the macro \n Once saved, the macro is available to agents from the Apply macro menu in the ticket editor. When an agent applies it, the comment (including the button) is inserted into the reply, ready to send. The button carries the same actions and behavior as any other Klick-Zen button: a customer click applies the configured actions to their ticket and redirects them to the landing page. \n \n Tips \n \n Modify the button wrapper HTML before pasting if it suits your use case; for example, you can use plain text as the hyperlink instead of an image, or wrap the hyperlink around your own hosted image \n Add more than one button to give the customer a choice (for example, \"Resolved\" and \"Still need help\"); create each button in Klick-Zen and paste both snippets into the same macro comment \n Combine the button with macro text so the reply reads naturally: a short explanation followed by the button gives the customer context for what they are being asked to do \n Chain actions with a trigger by having your button add a specific tag to a ticket when clicked, and then having a trigger fire when the tag is added; use this to route, reassign, or send a follow-up reply automatically after the customer clicks \n \n Troubleshooting \n \n The button shows up as raw code or plain text: Confirm you pasted the snippet into a Comment/description action in the macro editor, which is a rich text field. If the snippet appears as source code, the field may be in a plain-text mode; switch it back to rich text \n The button image doesn't appear in the delivered email: Some email clients block remote images until the recipient allows them. Klick-Zen hosts the button images; once the recipient loads images, the button displays normally \n The macro isn't available on a ticket: Check the macro's availability settings (for example, whether it is assigned to a specific group) and confirm the agent has access \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "DTS-918J-0F3-G0S", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "name": "Using Buttons within Zendesk"}, {"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/macros", "name": "Macros"}], "last_updated": "Oct 13, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Using Buttons in Macros", "Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen", "Step 2: Create or open a Zendesk macro", "Step 3: Add the comment action and paste the snippet", "Applying the macro", "Tips", "Troubleshooting"], "pageName": "Macros", "pathName": "Using-buttons-within-zendesk > Macros", "parentName": "Using Buttons within Zendesk", "relativePath": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/macros"}, {"id": "ZY5-419P-90W-AKW", "page": {"id": "ZY5-419P-90W-AKW", "name": "Help Center", "path": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/help-center", "slug": "help-center", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "ZY5-419P-90W-AKW", "slug": "help-center", "title": "Help Center", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-21 16:01:15", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "content": " Using Buttons in the Help Center Ticket View \n Klick-Zen buttons can appear on the page where a customer views their own ticket in your Zendesk Help Center. This lets a customer take action on their ticket directly from the portal: confirming a resolution, escalating, or choosing a next step, without needing to reply or contact an agent. \n This placement works only on the customer's ticket view (the request page ) in the Help Center, and the button must be added to the page's source code through the Guide theme editor. It is not added through the Klick-Zen app or a business rule. \n \n The request page is rendered by your Guide theme template, which uses Curlybars (handlebars) placeholders rather than the ticket placeholders used in triggers, automations, and macros. Because of this, the \"standard\" Klick-Zen snippet will not work on this page; ensure that you turn on the \"Help Center Links\" toggle as described in Step 1 / Item 3 below before you copy the snippet. \n \n Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen \n \n \n Open the Klick-Zen app from the left sidebar in Zendesk \n \n \n From the main left-hand menu, click Available KlickButtons \n \n \n To ensure that the correct link is selected, click the toggle \"Help Center Links\" so that it is on/active for this copy operation \n \n \n \n Find the button you want to use and click the copy action on the right-hand side of the HTML snippet; The button's HTML snippet is copied to your clipboard \n \n \n \n \n Step 2: Open the request page template in the theme editor \n \n \n In Zendesk, open Guide admin \n \n \n Click the Customize design (paintbrush) icon on the left-hand menu \n \n \n On the theme you want to edit, click the \" Customize\" link in the lower right-hand corner of the theme preview box \n \n \n \n Click the \"Edit code\" button in the lower right-hand corner of the window \n \n \n \n Open the request_page.hbs template (the customer's ticket view) \n \n \n Consider duplicating your live theme and editing the copy first, so you can preview the button before publishing it to customers. \n \n Step 3: Paste the snippet \n In request_page.hbs , find the location where you want the button to appear (for example, near the ticket comments or the ticket detail area. Paste the snippet from Step 1 into the template at that location: \n \n \n Zendesk placeholders resolve only inside the request page template . If you place the \"Help Center Links\" snippet on any other template page, the placeholders will not fill in, and the button will not link correctly. \n \n Step 5: Preview and Publish \n \n \n Preview the theme and open a ticket as a customer to confirm the button renders and links correctly: \n \n \n \n When you are satisfied, publish the theme. \n \n \n Once published, every customer viewing their own ticket will see the button, and it will be wired to that customer's specific request. \n \n \n Tips \n \n Modify the button wrapper HTML as needed; for example, wrap the link around your own hosted image, or use plain text as the hyperlink instead of the button image \n Add more than one button to present a choice (for example, \"Resolved\" and \"Still need help\"); paste both converted snippets into the template \n Conditionally show the button using the template's existing logic if you only want it to appear on certain tickets (for example, based on status), by placing the snippet inside the relevant Curlybars block \n \n \n Troubleshooting \n \n The button appears but the placeholders show as literal text: The snippet is not inside the request page template, or is in a context where Curlybars does not process it. Confirm it is placed within request_page.hbs \n The button image doesn't appear: Klick-Zen hosts the button images. Confirm the image URL in the snippet is intact and that your browser or theme is not blocking the remote image \n Changes don't show for customers: Make sure you published the theme after editing, and that you edited the theme that is actually live \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "DTS-918J-0F3-G0S", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "name": "Using Buttons within Zendesk"}, {"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/help-center", "name": "Help Center"}], "last_updated": "Aug 22, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Using Buttons in the Help Center Ticket View", "Step 1: Copy the button snippet from Klick-Zen", "Step 2: Open the request page template in the theme editor", "Step 3: Paste the snippet", "Step 5: Preview and Publish", "Tips", "Troubleshooting"], "pageName": "Help Center", "pathName": "Using-buttons-within-zendesk > Help-center", "parentName": "Using Buttons within Zendesk", "relativePath": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/help-center"}, {"id": "HPP-VMQB-ZDS-1NQ", "page": {"id": "HPP-VMQB-ZDS-1NQ", "name": "Online Template Generator", "path": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/online-template-generator", "slug": "online-template-generator", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "HPP-VMQB-ZDS-1NQ", "slug": "online-template-generator", "title": "Online Template Generator", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-13 16:46:37", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "content": " Klick-Zen Online Template Generator \n The free Zendesk email template generator writes a ready-to-paste Zendesk email with Klick-Zen buttons already laid out in it. You pick when the email goes out and what you want the customer to do, and the generator writes the subject, the message, and a set of buttons that fit. \n The buttons in the generated email start out as placeholders . To make them work, each one is linked to a real Klick-Zen button in your account. If you don’t have those buttons yet, the generator can give you a button file that creates all of them in Klick-Zen in one step, with their actions already set. \n \n \n \n The whole process looks like this: \n \n Build the email in the generator \n Download the buttons (skip this and Step 3 if you have already created the buttons) \n Import the buttons into Klick-Zen \n Paste each button’s link back into the generator \n Copy the finished HTML into a Zendesk trigger or automation, and add any suggested Zendesk triggers \n \n \n \n Step 1: Build the email in the generator \n Open the email template generator and choose the options on the left-hand side. The preview on the right updates as you go. \n \n When does the email go out? The moment in the ticket’s life the email is for: Ticket received , Pending reminder , Solved , CSAT survey , or Re-engagement \n What should the customer do? The question the buttons answer: Reply , Confirm it’s fixed , Pick a topic , Rate us , or Answer a question \n Tone: Friendly or Professional wording \n Or start from a ready-made template: instead of the choices above, start from one of the ready-made templates (approvals, refunds, escalations, appointment confirmations and more) \n \n Under More options you can also change the sign-off, choose whether the ticket number appears in the subject and body, and set the Button roundness : \n \n Use the Preview and HTML tabs to switch between the email as the customer will see it and the code you will paste into Zendesk. Check Show sample data to replace placeholders such as {{ticket.requester.first_name}} with example values in the preview. \n To see exactly what each button will do to the ticket, open What each button does under “Making it a working email”. It lists every button with its ticket actions, along with any Zendesk triggers the workflow needs. \n \n Already have the buttons you need in Klick-Zen? Skip ahead to Step 4 and paste their links in \n \n Step 2: Download the buttons \n Under Your Klick-Zen button links , click Download buttons : \n \n Your browser saves a button file named after the template, for example klick-zen-buttons-solved-confirm-it-s-fixed.kzb . The file contains one button for every button in the email, including: \n \n The button text, colors, and roundness \n The button’s actions (for example Set Ticket Status → Solved and Add Ticket Tag → customer_confirmed ) \n A Tag Exists exception that stops the button from acting a second time on the same ticket (see How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? ) \n \n The file contains the buttons only, not the email itself. Keep the generator open in your browser tab; you will come back to it in Step 4. \n Don’t open or edit the .kzb file. Klick-Zen will reject a file that has been changed. If you need different buttons, change your choices in the generator and download a new file \n Step 3: Import the buttons into Klick-Zen \n \n Open the Klick-Zen app from the left sidebar in Zendesk \n From the main left-hand menu, click Available KlickButtons \n Click Import buttons in the top-right corner, next to “Create new button”: \n \n \n \n Choose the .kzb file you downloaded in Step 2 \n \n Before you import, ensure that the toggle option “Help Center Links” is NOT toggled “on”. The import summary shows each button’s HTML in whichever form the toggle is set to, and Help Center links don’t work in emails. The \"Help Center Links\" toggle will automatically set itself to OFF when you begin a button import. \n The Import buttons window lists every button in the file, with a preview of the button and a short summary of what it does: \n \n \n Each button’s actions are shown as labels, such as Status → Solved or Tag customer_confirmed \n Exceptions are shown as Skip if tag ... ; the button takes no action when the ticket matches \n All buttons are selected by default. Uncheck any button you don’t want, or use Select all / Select none \n If a button with the same name already exists in your account, the new one is saved with a number added (for example Yes all set-1 ), so your existing buttons are never overwritten \n Anything Klick-Zen had to adjust during the import appears in a note at the bottom of the window \n \n Click Import (it shows the number of selected buttons, for example “Import 2 buttons”). \n \n When the import finishes, a summary window lists each new button with its HTML snippet and a copy icon: \n \n \n Leave this window open; these snippets are what you paste into the generator in the next step. If the import suggests any Zendesk triggers, they are listed here too. Click Done when you are finished. The new buttons now appear in Available KlickButtons like any other button, and you can edit them as usual. \n \n Imported buttons arrive with the generator’s colors, not the colors from your Default Button Template . To put them on brand, edit each button’s appearance after importing. The changes apply everywhere the button is used. \n \n How custom fields and statuses are matched \n Ticket fields and statuses are different in every Zendesk account, so the button file describes them by name and Klick-Zen matches them to your account during the import: \n \n Update Ticket Field actions (for example the Rate us buttons set a csat_rating field) are matched to one of your ticket fields by its title . The match ignores capitals, spaces, and punctuation, so a field titled CSAT Rating matches csat_rating . If no field matches, that one action is left off the button and the import note tells you which button it was. Create the field in Zendesk first, or edit the button after importing and add the action yourself \n Set Ticket Status actions are matched to your account’s default status for that category (Open, Pending, On-hold, or Solved), including custom ticket statuses \n \n If the generator’s “What each button does” list mentions a field (for example field csat_rating = good ), create that ticket field in Zendesk before you import, so the action is matched automatically. For drop-down fields, the values the button sets (such as good ) must match the field options’ tag values. \n \n Step 4: Paste each button’s link into the generator \n Back in the generator, under Your Klick-Zen button links , there is a box next to each button. Copy each button’s HTML from the import summary window (Step 3) and paste it into the box next to the matching button: \n \n As you paste, the HTML updates and the preview switches from the placeholder to your real Klick-Zen button image. Once every box has a link, the note above the boxes changes to “All buttons are linked.” \n You can also get a button’s HTML at any time from Available KlickButtons in Klick-Zen, by clicking the copy action next to the button’s HTML snippet. The generator accepts either the full HTML snippet or just the link URL. \n When copying from Available KlickButtons, ensure that the toggle option “Help Center Links” is NOT toggled “on”. Help Center links only work on the Help Center request page, not in emails. \n \n Step 5: Add the email to Zendesk \n \n \n Click Copy HTML under the preview \n \n \n \n The generator shows where the email belongs under Making it a working email . Emails sent when something happens to a ticket (received, solved, confirmed) go in a trigger ; emails sent after a period of time (pending for 48 hours, five days before closing) go in an automation \n \n \n In Zendesk Admin Center , go to Objects and rules → Business rules , open the trigger or automation, and paste the HTML into the Email body of its email action. Use the subject shown at the top of the generator’s preview as the Email subject \n \n \n For the full walkthrough, see Using Buttons in Triggers or Using Buttons in Automations . \n \n The generated HTML is a single line on purpose. Zendesk turns line breaks in an email body into extra blank space, so paste it as-is and don’t add line breaks inside it. \n \n \n Adding the suggested Zendesk triggers \n A button can update status, tags, fields, comments, priority, and type, but some workflows need more, such as assigning the ticket to a group or notifying a team lead. When that happens, the generator (under What each button does ) and the import summary list a suggested Zendesk trigger for each one, for example: \n \n When Tags contain at least one of: csat_bad Then Notify lead \n \n Create a trigger for each suggestion with that tag as its condition and the listed work as its actions. The button adds the tag, and your trigger does the rest. \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "DTS-918J-0F3-G0S", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk", "name": "Using Buttons within Zendesk"}, {"url": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/online-template-generator", "name": "Online Template Generator"}], "last_updated": "Oct 13, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Klick-Zen Online Template Generator", "The whole process looks like this:", "Adding the suggested Zendesk triggers"], "pageName": "Online Template Generator", "pathName": "Using-buttons-within-zendesk > Online-template-generator", "parentName": "Using Buttons within Zendesk", "relativePath": "/en/using-buttons-within-zendesk/online-template-generator"}, {"id": "ODA-HDVM-E24-ZAB", "page": {"id": "ODA-HDVM-E24-ZAB", "name": "Overview", "path": "/en/klick-zen-settings/overview", "slug": "overview", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "ODA-HDVM-E24-ZAB", "slug": "overview", "title": "Overview", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:14:00", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/klick-zen-settings", "content": " Klick-Zen Settings \n The Settings page holds the account-wide options that govern how Klick-Zen behaves across your Zendesk instance: how buttons can be used, how ticket updates are performed and attributed, how the service is branded, and where customers land after a click. Changes apply to all of your buttons, so it is worth reviewing these before rolling buttons out broadly. \n Each setting is explained below. When you finish making changes, click Update to save them, or Close to exit without saving. \n \n \n \n Allow KlickButtons in Ticket Editor \n Type: On/off toggle \n When enabled, Zendesk agents may insert individual KlickButtons into ticket messages using the Klick-Zen message tool within the Zendesk ticket editor. This is the toolbar feature that lets an agent add a button by hand while composing a reply. \n Turn this on if you want agents to place buttons into ad-hoc replies. Turn it off to remove the Klick-Zen tool from the ticket editor and limit buttons to automated channels such as triggers, automations, and macros. \n \n Ticket Editor Label \n Type: Dropdown (label) \n This setting controls which buttons agents can insert from the ticket editor, based on the button's labels. \n Left at \"Any label (all visible buttons)\" (the default), the ticket editor works exactly as before: agents can insert any enabled button that is set to show in the editor. When you choose a label, agents see only the buttons that carry that label . Each button must still be enabled and set to show in the ticket editor. \n The dropdown is grayed out while Allow KlickButtons in Ticket Editor is off. \n If the label you selected is later removed from every button, it appears as \"&lt;label&gt; (no button has this label)\" , and agents will see no buttons at all in the ticket editor. If agents report an empty button picker, check this setting first. \n \n Log Issue as Private Comment \n Type: On/off toggle \n When enabled, any issue that arises when a button click fails to update the ticket is logged within that same ticket as a private comment. Because the comment is private, it is visible to agents but never to the customer. \n A Zendesk tag titled \" KlickZen_exception \" will also be added to the ticket so that tickets with update exceptions can be easily identified. \n This is primarily a troubleshooting aid. If a click does not produce the expected change, the private comment gives your team an on-ticket record of what went wrong, right where the problem occurred. Leaving this enabled is recommended so failures are never silent. \n \n OAuth Account \n Type: Account connection \n This is the Zendesk user account Klick-Zen uses to connect to your instance and perform button actions. The page displays the current OAuth user account so you can see which account is in effect. \n All actions taken within Zendesk by Klick-Zen run in the security context of this OAuth user. The account must be a Zendesk administrator \n To change the account, use the Re-Assign OAuth Token button. The new OAuth user is set to the account you are currently logged in to Zendesk with , so before clicking, make sure you are logged in as the account you want Klick-Zen to use. If you want to switch to a different user, log out and log back in to Zendesk as that user (who must be a Zendesk administrator), then return to this page and re-assign. \n \n Comment Delegate Account \n Type: Dropdown (user account) \n When Klick-Zen enters a comment on a ticket, this setting controls which user the comment appears to come from. Select an account here to have button-generated comments attributed to that user. \n If left blank ( DEFAULT ), comments are attributed to the OAuth user account that was used to set up Klick-Zen. Use the delegate account when you would prefer button comments to appear under a dedicated or more customer-appropriate identity rather than the OAuth account itself. \n \n Host Mapping \n Type: Text field (domain name) \n Host Mapping lets you map one of your own domain names to Klick-Zen so buttons and landing pages are served from your domain instead of the default Klick-Zen host. For example, you might use klick.yourdomain.com in place of buttons.klick-zen.com . This gives customers a more seamless, on-brand experience, since button images, links, and actions are served from a domain they recognize. \n When you populate this field, Klick-Zen allows your custom domain to be used to view buttons and perform button actions. You will be presented with DNS values to add to your DNS settings for your domain; these values must be populated before a custom secure certificate can be created and applied to your custom domain: \n \n \n When you set a Host Mapping value, HTML snippets shown within the Klick-Zen interface are updated automatically to reflect the new URL, but any snippets already placed in your Zendesk email templates, macros, or Help Center pages will not update on their own. You must manually update those existing snippets to show the new value. The base domains of \"app.klick-zen.com\" and \"buttons.klick-zen.com\" will always function regardless of your custom domain setting. \n \n Default Post-Action URL \n Type: Text field (URL) \n This is the default web page a customer is redirected to after a button's action is completed (for example, https://www.YourCompany.com/LandingPage ). \n If this field is left blank, customers are sent to your Klick-Zen Default Landing Page instead. This value sets the account-wide default; it can be overridden within an individual button's settings when a specific button needs to send customers somewhere different. \n \n Billing \n Type: Button \n To view and modify your billing information, click View Billing Information . For security, your billing information opens in a new browser window . \n \n Saving Your Changes \n \n Update saves the changes you have made on this page. \n Close exits the Settings page. Be sure to click Update first if you want your changes kept. \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "7DN-IAAN-QO2-J2M", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/klick-zen-settings", "name": "Klick-Zen Settings"}, {"url": "/en/klick-zen-settings/overview", "name": "Overview"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Klick-Zen Settings", "Allow KlickButtons in Ticket Editor", "Ticket Editor Label", "Log Issue as Private Comment", "OAuth Account", "Comment Delegate Account", "Host Mapping", "Default Post-Action URL", "Billing", "Saving Your Changes"], "pageName": "Overview", "pathName": "Klick-zen-settings > Overview", "parentName": "Klick-Zen Settings", "relativePath": "/en/klick-zen-settings/overview"}, {"id": "AGV-PP9X-FJO-YSE", "page": {"id": "AGV-PP9X-FJO-YSE", "name": "Index", "path": "/en/how-do-i", "slug": "index", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "AGV-PP9X-FJO-YSE", "slug": "index", "title": "Index", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-31 21:37:09", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How Do I... \n The articles in this section cover the most common things people build, from a one-click ticket close to a full CSAT program. A few also go deeper into specific functionality, such as using your own domain name or controlling how button comments are attributed. Many share the same handful of ideas (tags, exceptions, landing pages, and follow-up triggers), so once a few patterns click into place, you will start spotting new possibilities of your own. \n Browse the topics below to get started: \n \n ...use my own button image ...use my own domain name ...prevent a button from being clicked ...enhance button actions by firing triggers \n ...use my own landing page ...ensure a button is only clicked once ...track visits to a web page ...create a one-click CSAT \n ...log button-click issues to the ticket ...troubleshoot a button ...present the customer with a choice ...build a one-click ticket close \n ...route a ticket based on customer choice ...update a ticket without logging in ...add a button to a follow up email ...change the button comment author \n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}], "last_updated": "Aug 31, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How Do I..."], "pageName": "How Do I...", "pathName": "How-do-i", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i"}, {"id": "7SK-SB2O-H89-E98", "page": {"id": "7SK-SB2O-H89-E98", "name": "...use my own button image", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-button-image", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-button-image", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "7SK-SB2O-H89-E98", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-button-image", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;use my own button image", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-31 21:30:59", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I use my own button image? \n Every Klick-Zen button snippet is just a hyperlink wrapped around an image. The link is what performs the ticket action; the image is only what the customer sees. That means you can replace the Klick-Zen button image with your own graphic while keeping the button fully functional. \n \n Your image must be hosted on the internet in order to be visible to your end user; Klick-Zen does not host your own button images. If you do not have the capability to host your own images, you can use an image hosting provider. Some image hosting providers (that have \"free\" tiers) include Postimages , ImgBB , and Linkyhost (these image hosting providers are not affiliated with Klick-Zen). \n \n The snippet structure \n A standard snippet looks like this: \n &lt;a href=\"https://app.klick-zen.com/ext/href/BUTTON_ID/{{ticket.id}}/{{ticket.requester.id}}/{{ticket.created_at_with_timestamp}}\"&gt;&lt;img src=\"https://app.klick-zen.com/ext/src/BUTTON_ID/{{ticket.id}}\" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt; \n \n The &lt;a href='...'&gt; portion is the action link. The &lt;img src='...'&gt; portion is the button image that Klick-Zen hosts and renders for you. \n \n \n Swap in your own image \n Replace the src value inside the &lt;img&gt; tag with the URL of your own hosted image. Leave the &lt;a href='...'&gt; action link exactly as Klick-Zen generated it: \n &lt;a href=\"https://app.klick-zen.com/ext/href/BUTTON_ID/{{ticket.id}}/{{ticket.requester.id}}/{{ticket.created_at_with_timestamp}}\"&gt;&lt;img src=\"https://www.yourcompany.com/images/your-button.png\" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt; \n When the customer clicks your image, the action link fires exactly as before and the configured ticket actions are applied. \n \n Host your image somewhere publicly reachable so email clients can load it and use an absolute URL (beginning with https:// ). Remember that many email clients block remote images until the recipient allows them, which applies to your image just as it does to a Klick-Zen-hosted one. \n \n Prefer a text link instead? \n You don't have to use an image at all. Remove the &lt;img&gt; tag and put your own text between the anchor tags to turn the button into a plain hyperlink: \n &lt;a href=\"https://app.klick-zen.com/ext/href/BUTTON_ID/{{ticket.id}}/{{ticket.requester.id}}/{{ticket.created_at_with_timestamp}}\"&gt;Click here to confirm&lt;/a&gt; \n The action link is the only essential part of the snippet. Everything wrapped inside it is presentation, and yours to control. \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-button-image", "name": "...use my own button image"}], "last_updated": "Aug 31, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I use my own button image?", "The snippet structure", "Swap in your own image", "Prefer a text link instead?"], "pageName": "...use my own button image", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-button-image", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-button-image"}, {"id": "TQY-SCS0-MN3-GX9", "page": {"id": "TQY-SCS0-MN3-GX9", "name": "...use my own domain name", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-domain-name", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-domain-name", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "TQY-SCS0-MN3-GX9", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-domain-name", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;use my own domain name", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-31 21:14:57", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I use my own domain name? \n By default, Klick-Zen serves button images, landing pages, and action links from a Klick-Zen host (for example, buttons.klick-zen.com ). Using the Host Mapping setting, you can serve them from one of your own domain names instead, so customers see a domain they recognize rather than a third-party one. This gives your buttons a more seamless, on-brand appearance. \n In order to use your own domain name to host images, you must have access to your DNS settings . If you do not know how to access your DNS settings for your domain, contact your system administrator or domain registrar. \n Setting this up has two parts: entering your domain in Klick-Zen, and adding matching DNS records at your domain provider. The DNS records must exist before you finalize the change, so it is easiest to work through the steps in order. \n \n Step 1: Choose your custom domain and view the required DNS records \n \n \n Open the Klick-Zen Settings page \n In the Host Mapping field, enter the domain (subdomain) you want to use, for example buttons.mydomain.com \n Klick-Zen displays the DNS entries that must exist for that domain before the mapping can take effect \n \n If you click the \"Update\" (Save) button before these two records are added to your DNS records, you will receive a message stating that the records have not been validated. Ensure that the required records are put in place before you click \"Update\". \n You will see two records to create, similar to this: \n Name Type Value buttons.mydomain.com CNAME buttons.klick-zen.com _acme-challenge.buttons.mydomain.com CNAME \"buttons.mydomain.com.abc123def456ghi789.dcv.cloudflare.com\" \n \n The first CNAME record points your subdomain at the Klick-Zen host that actually serves the buttons \n The second CNAME record is a validation record that proves you control the domain. Its value is unique to your domain, so use the exact value shown on your own settings page, not the example above \n \n \n Step 2: Add the DNS records at your domain provider \n \n Sign in to your DNS provider (wherever your domain's DNS is managed, for example your registrar or hosting provider) \n Create the CNAME records exactly as shown \n Save the records \n \n DNS changes can take anywhere from a few minutes to several hours to propagate, depending on your provider and the record's TTL. If validation does not succeed right away, allow some time and try again. \n Step 3: Save the Host Mapping in Klick-Zen \n Once the DNS records are in place and have had time to propagate, return to the Klick-Zen Settings page and click Update . \n Clicking Update performs the validation. Klick-Zen checks that the required DNS records exist and match before it will save the mapping, so the records must already be live in your DNS at that point. If validation fails, the mapping is not saved; confirm the CNAME and TXT records are correct and have had time to propagate, then click Update again. Once validation succeeds and the mapping is saved, Klick-Zen will allow your custom domain to be used to view buttons and perform button actions. \n \n Step 4: Update your existing snippets \n Existing snippets are not updated automatically in the links within your existing saved macros and triggers. When you set a Host Mapping value, the HTML snippets shown within the Klick-Zen interface change to reflect the new URL, but any snippets you have already placed in Zendesk email templates, macros, or Help Center pages will still point at the old host. You must manually update those existing snippets to show the new value. \"buttons.klick-zen.com\" will always work for your snippets. \n \n New snippets : copy them from Klick-Zen as usual; they will already carry your custom domain \n Existing snippets : edit each place you previously pasted a snippet (triggers, automations, macros, Help Center templates) and replace the old host with your new domain \n \n \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-domain-name", "name": "...use my own domain name"}], "last_updated": "Aug 31, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I use my own domain name?", "Step 1: Choose your custom domain and view the required DNS records", "Step 2: Add the DNS records at your domain provider", "Step 3: Save the Host Mapping in Klick-Zen", "Step 4: Update your existing snippets"], "pageName": "...use my own domain name", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-domain-name", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-domain-name"}, {"id": "OWW-NH0F-32S-FKI", "page": {"id": "OWW-NH0F-32S-FKI", "name": "...prevent a button from being clicked", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-prevent-a-button-from-being-clicked", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-prevent-a-button-from-being-clicked", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "OWW-NH0F-32S-FKI", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-prevent-a-button-from-being-clicked", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;prevent a button from being clicked", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-12 22:13:00", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I prevent a button from being clicked? \n If you want to switch a button off so that it no longer performs any action (even though it may already be sitting in emails your customers have received), use the Button Enabled checkbox within that button's settings. This is a simple on/off control for the entire button. \n \n \n Disable a button \n \n Open the button in Klick-Zen \n Uncheck Button Enabled \n Save the button \n \n The button is now disabled account-wide. \n \n What happens when a button is disabled \n Disabling a button takes effect everywhere immediately , including snippets already delivered in past emails. Specifically: \n \n The button appears greyscale. Instead of its normal colors, a disabled button renders in grey, giving the customer a visual cue that it is inactive \n Clicking takes no action on the ticket. No configured actions run when a disabled button is clicked \n The customer still reaches the landing page. A click redirects the customer to the button's landing page as usual; only the ticket actions are skipped \n \n This makes the checkbox useful for retiring a button, pausing a campaign, or taking a button out of service without having to hunt down and remove every snippet you have already placed in triggers, macros, or old emails. \n \n Re-enabling \n To turn the button back on, open its settings, re-check Button Enabled , and save. It returns to full color and resumes performing its actions on click. \n \n If instead of switching a button off entirely you want it to stop working only under certain conditions (for example, after it has already been clicked once), use Button Action Exceptions rather than the Button Enabled checkbox. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once ? \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-prevent-a-button-from-being-clicked", "name": "...prevent a button from being clicked"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I prevent a button from being clicked?", "Disable a button", "What happens when a button is disabled", "Re-enabling"], "pageName": "...prevent a button from being clicked", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-prevent-a-button-from-being-clicked", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-prevent-a-button-from-being-clicked"}, {"id": "ZUG-M53L-2PA-LJN", "page": {"id": "ZUG-M53L-2PA-LJN", "name": "...use buttons for internal routing and approvals", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-buttons-for-internal-routing-and-approvals", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-buttons-for-internal-routing-and-approvals", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "ZUG-M53L-2PA-LJN", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-buttons-for-internal-routing-and-approvals", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;use buttons for internal routing and approvals", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 20:44:26", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " \n How do I use buttons for internal routing and approvals? \n Klick-Zen buttons are not only for customers. Because a click applies ticket actions through the Zendesk API, a button placed in an internal note (or internal email) becomes a one-click control for your own team. Instead of a customer confirming a resolution, a manager can approve a refund, an agent can escalate a ticket, or a supervisor can sign off on a response, all from a button inside the ticket. \n The mechanics are the same as any other button. The difference is where you put it and who clicks it : internal-routing buttons go in a private comment or internal email, so only staff ever see them. \n \n The core pattern \n \n Create a button in Klick-Zen with the internal action you want, for example adding an approved tag, changing status, or reassigning the ticket \n Place the button's snippet in an internal note on the ticket (not a public reply), so it stays private to agents \n When a staff member clicks it, the button applies its actions, and any Zendesk trigger watching for those changes can carry the workflow forward \n \n Always add internal-routing buttons to private comments , never public replies. A button in a public reply is visible to the customer. Keeping these buttons internal ensures approvals and escalations stay between staff. \n \n Usage scenarios \n Refund approval \n An agent handling a refund request adds a button to an internal note and assigns the ticket to a manager. The manager opens the ticket and sees two buttons: \n \n Approve refund : adds tag refund_approved , assigns the ticket back to the original agent, adds a private comment recording the approval \n Deny refund : adds tag refund_denied , assigns the ticket back to the agent, adds a private comment noting the denial \n \n One click sends the decision and the ticket back to the agent, with a permanent record on the ticket of who decided what. \n \n Escalation to a manager \n An agent who needs a supervisor's involvement clicks an Escalate to manager button in an internal note: \n \n Adds tag escalated \n Raises priority \n Assigns the ticket to the escalation group \n \n A trigger watching for the escalated tag can then notify the on-call manager, so the escalation reaches the right person immediately. \n \n Response sign-off / QA \n For teams that require review before certain replies go out, a drafting agent leaves the reply ready and clicks a Request sign-off button in an internal note, routing the ticket to a reviewer. The reviewer clicks: \n \n Approved to send : tags qa_passed , assigns back to the agent \n Needs changes : tags qa_changes_requested , assigns back to the agent, and a trigger can add a task or comment prompting revisions \n \n \n Discount or exception authorization \n A frontline agent requests a one-time discount or a policy exception. An Authorize exception button in an internal note, clicked by a team lead, tags the ticket exception_approved and records the approval, so the agent has clear, logged authorization to proceed. \n \n Handoff between teams \n A button such as Send to Billing or Send to Engineering , placed in an internal note, reassigns the ticket to the right internal group and tags it accordingly, giving agents a consistent one-click handoff instead of manual reassignment. \n \n \n Make approvals auditable \n Pair each decision button with an Add comment (private) action so the ticket carries a written record of what was approved or denied. The tag each button adds is easy to report on in Zendesk Explore, giving you a clean audit trail of approvals, denials, and escalations over time. \n Tips \n \n Use distinct tags per decision ( refund_approved vs refund_denied ) so downstream triggers and reports can tell the outcomes apart \n Make internal buttons click-once with a tag and exception, so a decision cannot be accidentally clicked twice. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? \n Let triggers do the heavy lifting : keep each button simple (a tag, a status, a reassignment) and let Zendesk triggers handle notifications and routing based on the tags. See How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? \n Deliver the button by email if you prefer : because internal notes can also generate email notifications to assignees, a manager can receive the approval buttons in their inbox and click without opening Zendesk \n \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-buttons-for-internal-routing-and-approvals", "name": "...use buttons for internal routing and approvals"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I use buttons for internal routing and approvals?", "The core pattern", "Usage scenarios", "Make approvals auditable", "Tips"], "pageName": "...use buttons for internal routing and approvals", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-use-buttons-for-internal-routing-and-approvals", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-buttons-for-internal-routing-and-approvals"}, {"id": "LDG-E0LL-AEZ-RBI", "page": {"id": "LDG-E0LL-AEZ-RBI", "name": "...enhance button actions by firing triggers", "path": "/en/how-do-i/enhance-button-actions-by-firing-triggers", "slug": "enhance-button-actions-by-firing-triggers", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "LDG-E0LL-AEZ-RBI", "slug": "enhance-button-actions-by-firing-triggers", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;enhance button actions by firing triggers", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-12 22:37:14", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? \n A Klick-Zen button applies its actions to a ticket through the Zendesk API, which means those updates are indistinguishable from any other ticket change. As a result, your existing Zendesk triggers evaluate a button click just like they would an agent's edit. You can use this to extend a single click into a much larger workflow. \n \n Button adds a tag, trigger reacts \n The simplest and most reliable hook is a tag: \n \n \n In the button's Actions , use Add Ticket Tag with a tag of your choosing, for example customer_confirmed \n \n \n \n In Zendesk, create a trigger whose condition is Tags → Contains at least one of the following → customer_confirmed \n \n \n Give that trigger whatever actions you want to happen next \n \n \n When the customer clicks the button, Klick-Zen adds the tag, the trigger sees the tag, and your follow-up actions run automatically. \n \n What the follow-up trigger can do \n Because the follow-up is an ordinary Zendesk trigger, it can do anything a trigger can do, including: \n \n Route or reassign the ticket to a specific group or agent \n Send a follow-up email , such as a \"thanks for confirming\" message \n Change status, priority, or fields beyond what the button itself set \n Notify a team member or an external target \n \n Think of the button click as the customer-facing input and the trigger as the engine that reacts to it. A button might set only a tag and a status, while the trigger it fires handles routing, notifications, and messaging. This keeps each button simple and puts the heavier logic in Zendesk where it can also be fired by other (non Klick-Zen) Zendesk processes. \n \n Example: escalation on request \n \n Button (\"I still need help\"): adds tag needs_escalation , sets priority to High \n Trigger (condition: tag needs_escalation added): assigns the ticket to your escalation group and emails a supervisor \n \n One customer click raises the priority, routes the ticket, and alerts the right person, with no agent involvement. \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/enhance-button-actions-by-firing-triggers", "name": "...enhance button actions by firing triggers"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers?", "Button adds a tag, trigger reacts", "What the follow-up trigger can do", "Example: escalation on request"], "pageName": "...enhance button actions by firing triggers", "pathName": "How-do-ihance-button-actions-by-firing-triggers", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/enhance-button-actions-by-firing-triggers"}, {"id": "GJW-7CMV-N3K-YP9", "page": {"id": "GJW-7CMV-N3K-YP9", "name": "...use my own landing page", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-landing-page", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-landing-page", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "GJW-7CMV-N3K-YP9", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-landing-page", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;use my own landing page", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-12 22:40:54", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I use my own landing page? \n After a customer clicks a button and Klick-Zen applies the ticket actions, the customer's browser is redirected to a landing page. By default this is the Klick-Zen-hosted confirmation page, but you can send customers to a web page of your own instead, such as your support site, a knowledge base article, or a survey. \n You can set your own landing page in two places: a global default for all buttons, and a per-button override for a specific button. \n \n Option 1: Set a global default for all buttons \n \n \n Open the Klick-Zen Settings page \n \n \n In the Default Post-Action URL field, enter the URL you want customers redirected to (for example, https://www.yourcompany.com/thank-you ) \n \n \n \n Click Update to save \n \n \n Every button now redirects to this URL after its action completes, unless a specific button overrides it in its own button settings. \n If you leave the Default Post-Action URL blank, customers are sent to your Klick-Zen Default Landing Page instead. \n \n Option 2: Override the landing page for a single button \n If most buttons should use the default, but one needs to send customers somewhere different, set the landing page within that button's own settings . The per-button value takes precedence over the global Default Post-Action URL for that button only. \n This is useful when a particular button has its own natural destination, for example a \"Book a call\" button that should land on your scheduling page, while every other button uses the global default. \n Which takes priority? \n \n If a button specifies its own landing page, that URL is used \n Otherwise, the Default Post-Action URL from Settings is used \n If that is also blank, the Klick-Zen Default Landing Page is used \n \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-landing-page", "name": "...use my own landing page"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I use my own landing page?", "Option 1: Set a global default for all buttons", "Option 2: Override the landing page for a single button", "Which takes priority?"], "pageName": "...use my own landing page", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-landing-page", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-use-my-own-landing-page"}, {"id": "9AH-RCEA-MM9-2FF", "page": {"id": "9AH-RCEA-MM9-2FF", "name": "...ensure a button is only clicked once", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-ensure-a-button-is-only-clicked-once", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-ensure-a-button-is-only-clicked-once", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "9AH-RCEA-MM9-2FF", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-ensure-a-button-is-only-clicked-once", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;ensure a button is only clicked once", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-12 22:45:47", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? \n Buttons live in emails, and a customer can click one more than once, or return to an old email and click again days later. To make a button act only on its first click and do nothing on every click afterward, combine a tagging action with a matching exception. \n \n The setup \n \n \n Open the button in Klick-Zen \n \n \n In Actions , add Add Ticket Tag → button_clicked (in addition to whatever else you would like the button to do). This stamps the ticket the first time the button is clicked. \n \n \n \n In Action Exceptions , add an exception that checks for the tag button_clicked \n \n \n \n Save the button \n \n \n \n How it works \n When a customer clicks the button, Klick-Zen checks the exceptions before applying any actions: \n \n First click : the ticket does not yet have button_clicked , so no exception matches. All actions run, including the one that adds the button_clicked tag \n Every later click : the ticket now carries button_clicked , the exception matches, and none of the button's actions run \n \n The result is a button that fires exactly once per ticket. \n Use a distinct tag per button (for example, \" csat_clicked\" , \" confirm_clicked\" ) if you have several single-use buttons on the same ticket and want each to be independently one-time. A single shared tag would let the first button clicked block all the others. \n \n A note on multiple buttons \n If you present a choice, such as \"Resolved\" and \"Still need help,\" and want the customer to pick only one, give both buttons the same tag in their Add tags action and both an exception on that shared tag. The first click of either button tags the ticket, and after that neither button will act, so the customer's first choice stands. \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-ensure-a-button-is-only-clicked-once", "name": "...ensure a button is only clicked once"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I ensure a button is only clicked once?", "The setup", "How it works", "A note on multiple buttons"], "pageName": "...ensure a button is only clicked once", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-ensure-a-button-is-only-clicked-once", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-ensure-a-button-is-only-clicked-once"}, {"id": "SMF-MAOH-AKI-KXE", "page": {"id": "SMF-MAOH-AKI-KXE", "name": "...track visits to a web page", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-track-visits-to-a-web-page", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-track-visits-to-a-web-page", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "SMF-MAOH-AKI-KXE", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-track-visits-to-a-web-page", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;track visits to a web page", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-12 22:52:15", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I track visits to a web page? \n You can use a Klick-Zen button (or link) to record when a customer visits a particular web page. The idea is simple: point the button at your target page as its landing destination, and have the same button leave a record on the ticket confirming the click. When the button is used, the ticket shows that the customer went to that page. \n \n The setup \n \n \n Create a button in Klick-Zen for the page you want to track \n \n \n Set the button's landing page to your target page URL, so a click sends the customer there (see How do I use my own landing page? ) \n \n \n \n In the button's Actions , add a record of the visit. Either: \n \n Add tags with a tag such as visited_pricing_page , or \n \n Add a comment noting that the customer followed the link (a private comment keeps it internal) \n \n \n \n \n \n Save the button and place it in your email, macro, or reply \n \n \n \n How it works \n When the customer clicks the button: \n \n Klick-Zen applies the button's actions, tagging the ticket or adding the comment that records the visit \n The customer is redirected to your target page \n \n Because the action and the redirect both happen on the same click, the tag or comment on the ticket becomes your confirmation that the customer visited the page. \n \n Making the record useful \n \n Tags are the easiest to report on. A tag like visited_pricing_page can be counted in Zendesk Explore or used as a condition in a follow-up trigger (for example, notify sales when a customer visits the pricing page) \n Comments are better when you want the visit visible in the ticket's timeline for agents to see in context \n \n This is a click-level confirmation recorded on the ticket, not full web analytics. It tells you that the customer followed the button to the page, which is often exactly what you need for support and sales follow-up. For detailed on-page behavior, pair it with your normal web analytics on the destination page. \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-track-visits-to-a-web-page", "name": "...track visits to a web page"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I track visits to a web page?", "The setup", "How it works", "Making the record useful"], "pageName": "...track visits to a web page", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-track-visits-to-a-web-page", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-track-visits-to-a-web-page"}, {"id": "ODE-18Y6-GW7-3GB", "page": {"id": "ODE-18Y6-GW7-3GB", "name": "...create a one-click CSAT", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-create-a-one-click-csat", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-create-a-one-click-csat", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "ODE-18Y6-GW7-3GB", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-create-a-one-click-csat", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;create a one-click CSAT", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 19:50:14", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I create a one-click CSAT? \n Most CSAT surveys lose respondents at the click-through: rating you means clicking a link, loading a page, and filling out a form. With Klick-Zen you can put the rating buttons directly in the email , so a single click is the entire survey. Each button records the score on the ticket as a tag and/or a custom field, and the customer lands on a branded thank-you page. \n \n Two ways to build it \n Simple : Good / Bad (two buttons) \n Create two buttons and place them side by side in your satisfaction email: \n Button Actions \"Great\" Add tag csat_good ; set custom field csat_rating = good \"Not so good\" Add tag csat_bad ; set custom field csat_rating = bad; set status → Open \n \n \n Granular : 5-point scale (five buttons) \n Create five buttons such as \"1 - Very bad\" through \"5 - Great\". Each button sets a csat_rating custom field to its value and adds a matching tag ( csat_1 through csat_5 ). Style them as numbers or phrases. \n \n \n Where to place the buttons \n Put the rating buttons in the email that goes out when a ticket is solved . That can be a trigger email template, or part of your solve macro. \n If you also use a one-click ticket-close workflow, a clean pattern is: the customer clicks \"issue resolved,\" which fires a trigger that sends the CSAT email. The customer then rates with a single additional click. \n \n Close the loop on bad ratings automatically \n Because the rating lives on the ticket as a tag, your Zendesk triggers can act on it immediately (see How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? ): \n \n csat_bad added: reopen the ticket, set priority to High, notify a team lead, and send the customer a message letting them know a senior agent is reviewing the ticket \n csat_good added: optionally send a thank-you email, or invite the customer to leave a public review \n \n A poor rating stops being a number in a dashboard and becomes a recovery workflow that starts within seconds. \n \n \n Why do this instead of native Zendesk CSAT? \n Zendesk's built-in satisfaction feature works very well, but it is rigid: limited styling, Zendesk-controlled wording and layout, and depending on configuration it can involve a click-through to a page. Building CSAT with Klick-Zen gives you: \n \n Full control over appearance and wording \n Any scale you want (2-point, 5-point, or otherwise) \n Freedom of placement : any trigger, macro, or ad-hoc reply, not just the standard satisfaction email \n Follow-up automation driven by the rating tags \n Full reportability , since ratings are stored as tags and custom fields and appear in Zendesk Explore alongside the rest of your ticket data \n \n It also works on Zendesk plans and configurations where native CSAT is not available or does not fit your workflow. \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-create-a-one-click-csat", "name": "...create a one-click CSAT"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I create a one-click CSAT?", "Two ways to build it", "Where to place the buttons", "Close the loop on bad ratings automatically", "Why do this instead of native Zendesk CSAT?"], "pageName": "...create a one-click CSAT", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-create-a-one-click-csat", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-create-a-one-click-csat"}, {"id": "L7R-9JW6-H4F-A60", "page": {"id": "L7R-9JW6-H4F-A60", "name": "...log button-click issues to the ticket", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-log-button-click-issues-to-the-ticket", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-log-button-click-issues-to-the-ticket", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "L7R-9JW6-H4F-A60", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-log-button-click-issues-to-the-ticket", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;log button-click issues to the ticket", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 19:50:09", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I log button issues to a ticket? \n If a button click ever fails to update a ticket, Klick-Zen can record what happened directly on that ticket, so your team has an on-the-spot record to diagnose from. This is controlled by the Log Issue as Private Comment setting. \n Turn it on \n \n \n Open the Klick-Zen Settings page \n \n \n Enable Log Issue as Private Comment \n \n \n \n Click Update to save \n \n \n \n What it does \n When enabled, any issue that arises when a button click fails to update the ticket is logged within that same ticket as a private comment . Because the comment is private, it is visible to your agents but never to the customer. In the below example, the button has an exception in its rules that keeps the button from functioning when the tag \"ticket_solved_button_clicked\" already exists on the ticket: \n \n This puts the explanation exactly where the issue occurred: instead of a click failing silently, the affected ticket carries a note describing what went wrong, right in its conversation history. \n \n \n Using it to troubleshoot \n \n Enable the setting \n Reproduce the button click that isn't working \n Open the affected ticket and read the private comment Klick-Zen left \n Use what it reports to pinpoint the cause (for example a blocking exception, a permissions problem, or a field that could not be set) \n \n See How do I troubleshoot a button that isn't working? for the common causes and their fixes. \n &lt;div class=\"sd-callout\" data-callout-type=\"tip\"&gt;Leaving this setting enabled is recommended. It has no effect on successful clicks, and it means button failures are never silent, they always leave a trail on the ticket for your team to follow.&lt;/div&gt; \n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-log-button-click-issues-to-the-ticket", "name": "...log button-click issues to the ticket"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I log button issues to a ticket?", "Turn it on", "What it does", "Using it to troubleshoot"], "pageName": "...log button-click issues to the ticket", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-log-button-click-issues-to-the-ticket", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-log-button-click-issues-to-the-ticket"}, {"id": "WCX-H7SQ-KZS-BL2", "page": {"id": "WCX-H7SQ-KZS-BL2", "name": "...troubleshoot a button", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-troubleshoot-a-button", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-troubleshoot-a-button", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "WCX-H7SQ-KZS-BL2", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-troubleshoot-a-button", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;troubleshoot a button", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:38:41", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I troubleshoot a button that isn't working? \n Most button problems fall into a handful of causes. Work through the ones that match your symptom below. \n \n The button shows up as raw code or plain text \n The snippet was placed somewhere that does not render HTML. Confirm you pasted it into a surface that supports HTML: a trigger or automation email body, or a macro Comment/description field (a rich text field). Plain-text-only fields will display the snippet as code. \n \n The button image doesn't appear \n \n Blocked remote images. Many email clients hide remote images until the recipient allows them. Klick-Zen hosts the button image; once the recipient loads images, it appears \n Broken image URL. If you edited the snippet (for example to use your own image or a custom domain), confirm the &lt;img&gt; source is a valid, publicly reachable, absolute URL \n \n \n Extra spacing around the button \n Keep the snippet on a single line . Zendesk recommends minified, one-line HTML in trigger and automation bodies. Klick-Zen snippets are already single-line, so just avoid adding line breaks inside the pasted code. \n \n The button appears greyscale \n A greyscale button is disabled . Open the button in Klick-Zen and confirm Button Enabled is checked. A disabled button takes no ticket action on click and only sends the customer to the landing page. See How do I prevent a button from being clicked? . \n \n The click doesn't update the ticket \n \n An exception is blocking it. If the button has Action Exceptions, the ticket may match one, which stops all actions. Check whether the ticket already carries a blocking tag or state (for example a button_clicked tag on a click-once button) \n The button is disabled \n Check the private comment log. If Log Issue as Private Comment is enabled in Settings, a failed click is recorded as a private comment on that same ticket, which usually tells you exactly what went wrong. See How do I log button issues to a ticket? \n \n \n Agents see no buttons (or too few) in the ticket editor \n \n The Ticket Editor Label setting is filtering them. If Ticket Editor Label in Settings is set to a specific label, agents only see buttons carrying that label. If the chosen label is no longer on any button, it shows as \"&lt;label&gt; (no button has this label)\" and agents see nothing. Set it back to \"Any label (all visible buttons)\" or apply the label to the buttons that should appear. \n Editor KlickButtons are turned off. Confirm Allow KlickButtons in Ticket Editor is on in Settings. \n The button isn't set to show in the editor , or is disabled. Check the button's own settings. \n \n \n Placeholders show as literal text (Help Center) \n On the Help Center ticket view, the standard {{ticket...}} placeholders do not resolve. That page requires the request-page placeholders ( {{request.id}} , {{request.requester.id}} , {{request.created_at}} ) and the snippet must be inside the request page template. See Using Buttons on the Help Center Ticket View . \n \n Still stuck? Enable Log Issue as Private Comment in Settings, reproduce the click, and read the private comment it leaves on the ticket. If you need a hand, email support@klick-zen.com . \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-troubleshoot-a-button", "name": "...troubleshoot a button"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I troubleshoot a button that isn't working?", "The button shows up as raw code or plain text", "The button image doesn't appear", "Extra spacing around the button", "The button appears greyscale", "The click doesn't update the ticket", "Agents see no buttons (or too few) in the ticket editor", "Placeholders show as literal text (Help Center)"], "pageName": "...troubleshoot a button", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-troubleshoot-a-button", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-troubleshoot-a-button"}, {"id": "C88-9UEY-XDW-ARX", "page": {"id": "C88-9UEY-XDW-ARX", "name": "...present the customer with a choice", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-present-the-customer-with-a-choice", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-present-the-customer-with-a-choice", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "C88-9UEY-XDW-ARX", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-present-the-customer-with-a-choice", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;present the customer with a choice", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 19:49:59", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I present the customer with a choice? \n You can place more than one Klick-Zen button in a single email to give the customer a clear set of options. Each button carries its own actions, so the customer's choice determines what happens to the ticket, all from one click. \n \n The setup \n \n Create each option as its own button in Klick-Zen, with the actions appropriate to that choice \n Copy each button's snippet \n Paste the snippets side by side (or stacked) in the same email body, macro, or reply \n \n \n A classic example is a resolution check: \n \n Is your issue resolved? \n [ Yes, close my ticket ] [ No, I still need help ] \n \n \n \"Yes, close my ticket\" : sets status to Solved, adds a customer_confirmed tag \n \n \"No, I still need help\" : reopens the ticket, raises priority, adds a needs_help tag \n \n \n \n Make the choices mutually exclusive (optional) \n If you want the customer to pick only one option and have their first choice stand, give every button in the set the same tag in its \"Add tags\" action, plus an exception on that shared tag. The first click of any button tags the ticket, and the shared exception then blocks the others. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? . \n \n Act on the choice automatically \n Because each button can add a distinct tag, you can drive very different follow-up workflows from a single email. A downstream Zendesk trigger watching for each tag can route, notify, or reply based on what the customer chose. See How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? . \n \n Style the options so the choice reads at a glance, for example a green \"Resolved\" button next to a red \"Still need help\" button. Klick-Zen gives you full control over each button's colors, font, and shape. \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-present-the-customer-with-a-choice", "name": "...present the customer with a choice"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I present the customer with a choice?", "The setup", "Make the choices mutually exclusive (optional)", "Act on the choice automatically"], "pageName": "...present the customer with a choice", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-present-the-customer-with-a-choice", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-present-the-customer-with-a-choice"}, {"id": "IAP-TZBZ-TV7-MGP", "page": {"id": "IAP-TZBZ-TV7-MGP", "name": "...build a one-click ticket close", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-build-a-one-click-ticket-close", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-build-a-one-click-ticket-close", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "IAP-TZBZ-TV7-MGP", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-build-a-one-click-ticket-close", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;build a one-click ticket close", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 19:53:26", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I build a one-click ticket close? \n A common support pattern is asking the customer to confirm their issue is resolved. With Klick-Zen, you can put a single button in the email that closes the ticket the moment the customer clicks it, with no reply and no login required. \n \n The setup \n \n Create a button in Klick-Zen, for example labeled \"Yes, my issue is resolved\" \n \n In the button's Actions , add: \n \n Set status → Solved (the core action that closes the ticket) \n Optionally, Add tags with a tag such as customer_closed , so you can report on or react to customer-initiated closes \n Optionally, Add a comment noting the customer confirmed resolution, for the ticket record \n \n \n Place the button in the email that asks the customer to confirm (a trigger email or your solve macro) \n \n When the customer clicks, the ticket moves to Solved and any tags or comments you configured are applied, all in one click. \n \n Make it click-once \n To prevent a second click (or a click on an old email) from disturbing an already-closed ticket, add a button_clicked tag as an action and an exception that checks for it. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? . You can also add an exception for tickets that are already Solved or Closed. \n \n Chain it into other workflows \n Because the close is an ordinary ticket update, your Zendesk triggers can react to it. A popular pattern is to have the resolved-confirmation click fire a follow-up trigger that sends a CSAT email , turning one click into a smooth \"confirm, then rate\" flow. See How do I create a one-click CSAT? and How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? . \n \n Pair the close button with a second \"No, I still need help\" button to give the customer a clear choice. See How do I present the customer with a choice? \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-build-a-one-click-ticket-close", "name": "...build a one-click ticket close"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I build a one-click ticket close?", "The setup", "Make it click-once", "Chain it into other workflows"], "pageName": "...build a one-click ticket close", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-build-a-one-click-ticket-close", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-build-a-one-click-ticket-close"}, {"id": "IMS-PNIT-WK1-SR8", "page": {"id": "IMS-PNIT-WK1-SR8", "name": "...route a ticket based on customer choice", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-route-a-ticket-based-on-customer-choice", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-route-a-ticket-based-on-customer-choice", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "IMS-PNIT-WK1-SR8", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-route-a-ticket-based-on-customer-choice", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;route a ticket based on customer choice", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 19:55:27", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I route a ticket based on the customer's choice? \n You can let customers route their own ticket by giving them buttons that represent the options, then having Zendesk send the ticket to the right place based on which one they click. The customer does the categorizing for you, in one click, and the ticket lands with the correct team automatically. \n \n The setup \n \n Create one button per destination or category; for example \"Billing question,\" \"Technical issue,\" and \"General inquiry\" \n In each button's Actions , add Add tags with a tag that identifies the choice (for example route_billing , route_technical , route_general ). You can also set a group or field directly in the button if you prefer \n Place the buttons together in the email so the customer can pick one (see How do I present the customer with a choice? ) \n \n In Zendesk, create a trigger for each route: \n \n Condition : the ticket has the matching tag (for example route_billing ) \n Action : assign the ticket to the appropriate group or agent, and set any fields you need \n \n \n \n When the customer clicks their choice, the button tags the ticket, the matching trigger fires, and the ticket is routed, with no agent triage required. \n \n Why route with a tag and trigger \n Adding a tag and letting a trigger act on it keeps the routing logic in Zendesk, where it is easy to see and maintain. It also means the same button can drive more than routing: the trigger it fires can notify a team lead, set priority, or send an acknowledgment at the same time. See How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? . \n \n Consider making these buttons click-once (a shared tag plus an exception) so a customer's first choice stands and a later click cannot re-route an in-progress ticket. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-route-a-ticket-based-on-customer-choice", "name": "...route a ticket based on customer choice"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I route a ticket based on the customer's choice?", "The setup", "Why route with a tag and trigger"], "pageName": "...route a ticket based on customer choice", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-route-a-ticket-based-on-customer-choice", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-route-a-ticket-based-on-customer-choice"}, {"id": "IB7-16QC-TQG-LXM", "page": {"id": "IB7-16QC-TQG-LXM", "name": "...update a ticket without logging in", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-update-a-ticket-without-logging-in", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-update-a-ticket-without-logging-in", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "IB7-16QC-TQG-LXM", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-update-a-ticket-without-logging-in", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;update a ticket without logging in", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 19:56:52", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I let customers update a ticket without logging in? \n No-login updates are the core of what Klick-Zen does. A button in an email lets the customer change their ticket, confirm a resolution, escalate, pick a category, rate their experience, with a single click and without signing in to your help center or replying to the email. \n \n Why no login is needed \n The button's link points to Klick-Zen, not to a page the customer has to authenticate into. When they click: \n \n Their browser opens the button's action link \n Klick-Zen applies the configured actions to the ticket over its own authorized connection to your Zendesk instance \n The customer is redirected to a confirmation landing page \n \n The customer never enters credentials. The authority to update the ticket comes from Klick-Zen's connection to Zendesk (set up once by an administrator), not from the customer's own login. See How Klick-Zen Works for the full sequence. \n \n What customers can do without logging in \n Any action you can assign to a button, including: \n \n Confirm resolution and close the ticket \n Reopen or escalate when they still need help \n Choose a category so the ticket routes to the right team \n Rate their experience in a one-click CSAT \n Acknowledge a message or confirm they visited a page \n \n \n Keeping it safe \n Each button link is specific to the ticket it was sent for, so a click acts only on that customer's own ticket. To prevent repeat or stale clicks, make buttons click-once with a tag and exception (see How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? ), and add exceptions for ticket states where an update should no longer be allowed. \n \n Because there is no login step, response rates on button prompts tend to be far higher than on flows that require the customer to open a portal or sign in. Removing friction is the whole point. \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-update-a-ticket-without-logging-in", "name": "...update a ticket without logging in"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I let customers update a ticket without logging in?", "Why no login is needed", "What customers can do without logging in", "Keeping it safe"], "pageName": "...update a ticket without logging in", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-update-a-ticket-without-logging-in", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-update-a-ticket-without-logging-in"}, {"id": "G7Z-MQ7A-MOB-YUD", "page": {"id": "G7Z-MQ7A-MOB-YUD", "name": "...add a button to a follow up email", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-add-a-button-to-a-follow-up-email", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-add-a-button-to-a-follow-up-email", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "G7Z-MQ7A-MOB-YUD", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-add-a-button-to-a-follow-up-email", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;add a button to a follow up email", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 20:00:29", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I add a button to a follow-up email? \n Follow-up emails, sent when a ticket is solved (or some time thereafter), are one of the best places for a Klick-Zen button. The customer gets a prompt to confirm, rate, or re-engage exactly when it is relevant, and they can act in one click. \n \n Solved-ticket follow-up (trigger) \n To send a button the moment a ticket is solved, use a trigger : \n \n Copy your button snippet from Klick-Zen \n In Zendesk, create or open a trigger with a condition like Status → changed to → Solved \n Add the action Notify by → User email , recipient Ticket → requester \n Paste the button snippet into the email body \n \n See the full walkthrough in Using Buttons in Trigger Emails . \n \n Delayed follow-up (automation) \n To send a button some time after an event (for example 24 hours after a ticket goes pending, or three days after it is solved), use an automation , which runs on a schedule: \n \n Copy your button snippet from Klick-Zen \n Create an automation with a time-based condition such as Hours since solved → (calendar) → is → 72 \n Add a tag action (for example followup_sent ) and an exception-style condition ( Tags → contains none of → followup_sent ) so it sends only once \n Add the email action and paste the button snippet into the body \n \n See the full walkthrough in Using Buttons in Automations . \n \n Good follow-up buttons \n \n \"Did we resolve your issue?\" with Yes / No buttons (see present the customer with a choice ) \n A one-click CSAT rating (see How do I create a one-click CSAT? ) \n \"Reopen my ticket\" for a delayed check-in after a solve \n \n \n Make follow-up buttons click-once and add an exception for already-closed tickets, so a customer clicking an older follow-up email cannot disturb a ticket that has since been resolved. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-add-a-button-to-a-follow-up-email", "name": "...add a button to a follow up email"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I add a button to a follow-up email?", "Solved-ticket follow-up (trigger)", "Delayed follow-up (automation)", "Good follow-up buttons"], "pageName": "...add a button to a follow up email", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-add-a-button-to-a-follow-up-email", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-add-a-button-to-a-follow-up-email"}, {"id": "EFE-FNP8-942-6TU", "page": {"id": "EFE-FNP8-942-6TU", "name": "...change the button comment author", "path": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-change-the-button-comment-author", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-change-the-button-comment-author", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "EFE-FNP8-942-6TU", "slug": "dot-dot-dot-change-the-button-comment-author", "title": "&#46;&#46;&#46;change the button comment author", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-14 20:05:45", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/how-do-i", "content": " How do I change which user button comments appear to come from? \n When a button adds a comment to a ticket, that comment is attributed to a user. By default, it comes from the account Klick-Zen used to connect to Zendesk (the OAuth user). If you would rather have button comments appear under a different, more appropriate account, use the Comment Delegate Account setting. \n \n Set the comment author \n \n Open the Klick-Zen Settings page \n Find Comment Delegate Account \n In the User Account dropdown, select the account you want button comments to appear from \n \n Click Update to save \n \n \n \n From then on, comments Klick-Zen enters into tickets will show as coming from the selected account. \n \n \n Default behavior \n If you leave the setting on DEFAULT , comments are attributed to the OAuth user account that was used to initially set up Klick-Zen. \n \n \n When to use a delegate account \n \n A customer-appropriate identity. If your OAuth account is an admin or service account, you may prefer public comments to appear under a friendlier support identity \n Consistency. Route all button-generated comments through a single, recognizable account so agents can tell at a glance which comments came from a button click \n \n This setting controls who button comments appear to come from. It is separate from the OAuth Account , which is the administrator account Klick-Zen uses to actually perform ticket actions. \n\n\n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "ULK-L43S-5YQ-JSH", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/how-do-i", "name": "How Do I..."}, {"url": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-change-the-button-comment-author", "name": "...change the button comment author"}], "last_updated": "Aug 14, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["How do I change which user button comments appear to come from?", "Set the comment author", "Default behavior", "When to use a delegate account"], "pageName": "...change the button comment author", "pathName": "How-do-i > Dot-dot-dot-change-the-button-comment-author", "parentName": "How Do I...", "relativePath": "/en/how-do-i/dot-dot-dot-change-the-button-comment-author"}, {"id": "MAJ-YCU4-TVY-IGA", "page": {"id": "MAJ-YCU4-TVY-IGA", "name": "Index", "path": "/en/frequently-asked-questions", "slug": "index", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "MAJ-YCU4-TVY-IGA", "slug": "index", "title": "Index", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-10-30 20:40:50", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/frequently-asked-questions", "content": " Frequently Asked Questions \n General \n Can't Zendesk do this natively? Not in the same way. Zendesk can send automated emails, but it can't put a functional, one-click action button inside them that updates the ticket when the customer clicks. That is exactly what Klick-Zen adds. \n Do I need a developer to use Klick-Zen? No. Buttons are created in the Klick-Zen app and added to Zendesk by pasting a snippet into a trigger, automation, or macro. No coding is required. The one place you work with template code is the Help Center ticket view, and even there you are only pasting a provided snippet. \n What ticket actions can a button perform? A button can update the ticket status, add tags, add a public or private comment, assign the ticket to an agent or group, change the priority, and change the ticket type. See Button Actions . \n Can one button perform multiple actions? Yes. A single button can carry several actions, and all of them are applied to the ticket in one pass when the customer clicks. For example, one click can set the ticket to Solved, add a tag, and post a comment. \n Is any additional hosting required? No. Klick-Zen hosts your button images and the post-click landing page for you, so there is nothing extra to set up or maintain. \n \n Appearance and customization \n Can I customize how buttons and the landing page look? Yes. You control each button's text, font, colors, border, and roundness, and you can brand the hosted landing page with your colors, logo, and message. See Button Appearance and Landing Pages . \n Can I use my own image or a plain text link instead of the Klick-Zen button? Yes. The snippet is just a link wrapped around an image, so you can swap in your own hosted image, or remove the image and use a text link. The action still fires on click. See How do I use my own button image? . \n Can I serve buttons from my own domain? Yes, using the Host Mapping setting. Once you add the required DNS records and validate the domain, buttons and links are served from your subdomain instead of the default Klick-Zen host. See How do I use my own domain name? . \n \n Actions, triggers, and workflows \n Does this work with my existing Zendesk triggers and automations? Yes, and this is where a lot of the power is. A button click updates the ticket through the Zendesk API, so it is treated exactly like any other ticket change. Your existing triggers and automations evaluate it normally, which means a button can add a tag and a downstream trigger can route, notify, or reply based on that tag. See How do I enhance button actions by firing triggers? . \n Can a button change the assignee or priority directly? Yes. Assigning the ticket and changing priority are both built-in button actions, so a button can reassign or re-prioritize a ticket on click without any additional setup. \n What happens if the customer never clicks a button? Nothing changes. A button is optional for the customer. The ticket simply stays as it is, and your normal workflow continues, so there is no downside to including one. \n Can I make a button work only once? Yes. Add an action that applies a tag (for example button_clicked ) and an exception that checks for that same tag. The first click applies the tag and runs the actions; every later click matches the exception and does nothing. See How do I ensure a button is only clicked once? . \n Can I stop a button from working? Yes. Uncheck Button Enabled in the button's settings. A disabled button renders in greyscale, takes no action when clicked, and simply sends the customer to the landing page. See How do I prevent a button from being used? . \n \n Permissions and limits \n Who can create or change buttons? Only Zendesk admins can create, edit, clone, delete or import buttons, change Settings, or open billing. Agents can still insert existing buttons from the ticket editor. \n Is there a limit to how many buttons I can have? Yes. An account can have up to 100 buttons . Deleted buttons don't count toward the limit, so removing buttons you no longer use frees up room. Each button can also have up to 6 actions and 6 exceptions. \n Can I organize my buttons once I have a lot of them? Yes. Give buttons labels and use the search, label filter, action/exception filter and sort tools on the button list. A label can also drive which buttons agents see in the ticket editor. \n \n Security and access \n Is it secure to let customers update tickets without logging in? Yes. Each button link is unique to a single ticket and is delivered only in that ticket's email thread, the same trust model as replying to a Zendesk email, which already updates the ticket. A click can only perform the specific actions you configured; it cannot read ticket contents, reach other tickets, or do anything you did not set up. \n Does the customer need a Help Center account or login to click a button? No. The button works straight from the email with no login and no Help Center account. See How do I let customers update a ticket without logging in? . \n What if the customer forwards the email to someone else? Then that person can click the button, the same way they could forward the email and reply to update the ticket that way. The click carries the same trust as the email thread it arrived in. Where that matters, such as approvals or anything financial, pair the button with a check your process already trusts rather than relying on the click alone. \n Is this the same as two-factor authentication? No. Klick-Zen is not an identity-verification or 2FA product. It gives the recipient of a ticket's email a trusted, one-click way to act on that ticket. For actions that require stronger assurance, combine the button with your own verification step. \n What gets recorded on the ticket when a button is clicked? Whatever actions you configured: any tags, comments, or field changes the button applies appear on the ticket like any other update. If you enable Log Issue as Private Comment , a failed click also leaves a private comment explaining what went wrong. \n Can an email security scanner or link prefetching trigger a button? No. Many email systems open links automatically, either security scanners checking for threats or email clients prefetching links before anyone clicks. Klick-Zen recognizes these automated visits and ignores them. A button's actions run only when a person actually clicks it, so a ticket won't be closed, rated, or rerouted just because an email was delivered or scanned. \n Do I need to allowlist Klick-Zen in our email security tools? Usually not. Scanners and prefetchers can open button links as usual; they just don't run the button's actions. If your security team prefers links that point to a domain you already trust, set up a custom domain (see below), and button links and images will come from your own company domain instead of Klick-Zen's. \n Can button links and images use our own domain? Yes. Point a subdomain of your company domain (for example, buttons.yourcompany.com) at Klick-Zen with a CNAME record, and your button links, button images and hosted landing page are served from it. Customers see a link from a domain they recognize, and your email security team only has to trust a domain you own. See Custom domain setup for the steps. \n \n Reporting \n Can I report on button activity in Zendesk Explore? Yes. Because buttons apply tags and field changes to the ticket, that data flows into Zendesk Explore alongside the rest of your ticket data. Tagging each outcome (for example csat_good , refund_approved , route_billing ) gives you clean, reportable results. \n \n Common use cases \n Can I build a CSAT survey with buttons? Yes. Place rating buttons directly in the solved-ticket email so the customer rates in one click, with no separate survey page. Each button records the score as a tag and, if you like, a custom field. See How do I create a one-click CSAT? . \n Can I use my own rating scale or button styles for CSAT? Yes. Because you design the buttons, you can use a 2-point, 5-point, or any other scale, styled however you want. This is a common reason teams choose Klick-Zen CSAT over the native satisfaction rating. \n Can a bad rating automatically trigger a follow-up? Yes. Have the low-rating button add a tag such as csat_bad , then use a Zendesk trigger on that tag to reopen the ticket, raise priority, and notify a lead. The rating stops being just a number and becomes a recovery workflow. \n What if the customer picks the wrong category when self-routing? The same as today, only sooner and cheaper to fix. An agent reassigns the ticket as they would any misrouted request, and the tag shows it happened, so if one category is chosen incorrectly often, you will see it in Explore and can reword the button. In practice, customers categorize their own requests more accurately than keyword rules do. \n Won't customers click \"escalate\" on everything? In practice, no, and even if a few do, you are no worse off than a customer replying \"this is urgent\" today. The difference is that the click is structured: it tags and routes the ticket so your team can see and manage escalations, rather than scanning replies for urgency. You can also add exceptions so an escalate button does nothing on tickets that are already escalated or closed. \n \n Internal and approval workflows \n Can I use buttons internally, for staff approvals? Yes. Place a button in an internal note and it becomes a one-click control for your team, for approvals, escalations, sign-offs, or handoffs, that the customer never sees. See How do I use buttons for internal routing and approvals? . \n Can I require manager approval before something proceeds? Yes, and the workflow enforces it rather than relying on memory. Route requests to a holding view, have the approval button add a tag such as approved , and use a trigger that releases the ticket to the next step only when that tag appears. The approval is recorded on the ticket as a timestamped private note. \n Is a button click enough for an approval audit trail? The click itself is one signal; pair it with a private comment action so each decision is written to the ticket, optionally under a specific comment delegate account . Combined with the tag each button adds, you get a reportable, timestamped record of who approved or denied what. \n \n Billing-related buttons \n Can a button actually cancel or change a subscription in my billing system? Not on its own. A button acts on the Zendesk ticket. It can capture the customer's intent (for example tag the ticket renewal_confirmed or cancel_requested ) and route it, but it does not reach into your billing system unless you connect that step yourself through your own trigger or integration. \n Does Klick-Zen connect to our billing or other external systems? Klick-Zen performs actions within Zendesk. To extend a click into another system, use the tag or ticket change the button makes as the hook for your existing Zendesk integrations or automations. \n \n Still have a question? See the How Do I...? guides or contact support \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "L0O-7XOE-PFT-F5E", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/frequently-asked-questions", "name": "Frequently Asked Questions"}], "last_updated": "Oct 30, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Frequently Asked Questions", "General", "Appearance and customization", "Actions, triggers, and workflows", "Permissions and limits", "Security and access", "Reporting", "Common use cases", "Internal and approval workflows", "Billing-related buttons"], "pageName": "Frequently Asked Questions", "pathName": "Frequently-asked-questions", "parentName": "Frequently Asked Questions", "relativePath": "/en/frequently-asked-questions"}, {"id": "B5Y-DEQ-AAE-VNM", "page": {"id": "B5Y-DEQ-AAE-VNM", "name": "Billing", "path": "/en/your-account/billing", "slug": "billing", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "B5Y-DEQ-AAE-VNM", "slug": "billing", "title": "Billing", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-18 18:26:24", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/your-account", "content": " Billing \n Klick-Zen is billed monthly. Billing is set up when you install Klick-Zen from the Zendesk Marketplace, and payments are processed securely by payment-industry leader Stripe . Klick-Zen is a product of Acenerate LLC , which is the name that appears on your invoices and receipts. \n \n Free trial \n Klick-Zen includes a 14-day free trial , so you can build and test buttons before you are charged. Billing does not begin until the trial ends; your first monthly charge occurs at the close of the 14-day trial period. \n \n Billing setup \n When you first install the full version of Klick-Zen, you will be prompted to enter billing information. Only credit card payments are accepted during initial setup. \n \n Monthly payments and statements \n Klick-Zen charges monthly for service. By default, the credit card entered during setup is the card charged each month. \n The email address of the Zendesk user who initiated the installation receives an emailed copy of the receipt each month once billing completes. Invoices and receipts are marked as being from Acenerate LLC . \n \n If a payment fails \n If a monthly charge does not go through (for example, an expired or declined card), Stripe will attempt the charge again over the following days. To avoid any interruption to your service, keep your card details current in the billing portal (see below). If you receive a failed-payment notice, updating your card is usually all that is needed to resolve it. \n \n Changing billing information \n Using your billing email address (the same address that receives your Klick-Zen invoices and statements), you can connect to your billing portal at https://www.klick-zen.com/billing/ . \n From the portal you can update your card, review past invoices, and manage your payment details. \n \n Cancellation \n We hate to see you go, but cancelling Klick-Zen is simple. Just cancel your subscription in the billing portal mentioned above, or uninstall the Klick-Zen app from Zendesk, and your billing account is automatically terminated. No further charges are made after you uninstall. \n Cancellation takes effect at the end of your current billing period: your service continues through the period you have already paid for, and monthly charges are not refundable or prorated. If you ever wish to use Klick-Zen again, simply reinstall the app from the Zendesk Marketplace. \n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "H1S-CB9-S0C-ZTT", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/your-account", "name": "Your Account"}, {"url": "/en/your-account/billing", "name": "Billing"}], "last_updated": "Aug 18, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Billing", "Free trial", "Billing setup", "Monthly payments and statements", "If a payment fails", "Changing billing information", "Cancellation"], "pageName": "Billing", "pathName": "Your-account > Billing", "parentName": "Your Account", "relativePath": "/en/your-account/billing"}, {"id": "SWF-Z0LP-JJ7-V4B", "page": {"id": "SWF-Z0LP-JJ7-V4B", "name": "Support", "path": "/en/your-account/support", "slug": "support", "tags": [], "type": "page", "yaml": {"id": "SWF-Z0LP-JJ7-V4B", "slug": "support", "title": "Support", "isVisible": true, "lastUpdated": "2026-08-18 18:31:26", "isSearchable": true}, "parent": "/your-account", "content": " Klick-Zen Support \n Need a hand with Klick-Zen? Most questions can be answered right here in the documentation, and anything else is one email away. \n \n Try the documentation first \n Many common questions are already covered, and self-service is usually the fastest path to an answer: \n \n Something not working? Start with How do I troubleshoot a button that isn't working? . It covers the most frequent issues: buttons showing as raw code, images not appearing, extra spacing, greyscale (disabled) buttons, and clicks that don't update the ticket \n Setting something up? The How Do I...? section walks through the most common tasks, from building a one-click CSAT to serving buttons from your own domain \n Diagnosing a failed click? Enable Log Issue as Private Comment in Settings, reproduce the click, and read the private comment Klick-Zen leaves on the ticket. It usually explains exactly what went wrong \n \n \n Contact us \n If the docs don't resolve it, email is the best way to reach us and we're glad to help: \n support@klick-zen.com \n We handle support over email, and we use Zendesk ourselves to manage it, so your request lands in a real ticketing system and nothing slips through the cracks. \n \n What to include \n To help us resolve your issue as quickly as possible, please include: \n \n Your Zendesk subdomain (for example, yourcompany.zendesk.com ) \n A description of the button or workflow involved \n What you expected to happen, and what actually happened \n Any screenshots that show the problem \n For a click that didn't update a ticket: the text of the private comment left on the ticket when Log Issue as Private Comment is enabled \n \n\n\n", "children": [], "isVisible": true, "parent_id": "H1S-CB9-S0C-ZTT", "breadcrumbs": [{"url": "/en/your-account", "name": "Your Account"}, {"url": "/en/your-account/support", "name": "Support"}], "last_updated": "Aug 18, 2026", "related_pages": []}, "tags": ["Klick-Zen Support", "Try the documentation first", "Contact us"], "pageName": "Support", "pathName": "Your-account > Support", "parentName": "Your Account", "relativePath": "/en/your-account/support"}];
